{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'for d in cores cores/bench; do if [ -f \"$d/AGENTS.md\" ]; then cat \"$d/AGENTS.md\"; fi; done; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +657.59%): Moving combinational ALU `/` and `%` into a shared four-bit-per-cycle, eight-cycle divider removes full-division depth from the EX timing path, letting clock-rate gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.25%): A 64-entry BHT plus early JAL cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45% at nearly unchanged Fmax (5.514 MHz), but lost to iterative division because prediction left the combinational DIV/REM timing bottleneck intact.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +11.77%): A narrow four-bit launch prefix lets quotient_q/remainder_q isolate the final restoring steps from sign correction and EX/MEM selection without delaying the t8 result handshake, improving timing without extra divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (regression, -0.69%): The 64-entry BHT plus early JAL reduced CoreMark cycles by 6.45%, but a 7.1% Fmax loss (41.241 to 38.315 MHz) outweighed the savings with the worst path still through divider quotient_q and EX, so predictor integration must preserve timing outside the fetch path.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +13.49%): Keeping raw fetch data and ID/EX payload enables independent of redirect, while squashing via validity and gating side effects locally, removes late branch feedback through register-file reads and load-use logic without adding a pipeline stage.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 (regression, +11.88%): Isolating recovery removed IF/hazard feedback, but the remaining register-file-to-EX/ALU-to-dmem timing path limited the BHT/early-JAL variant to 48.244 MHz, so its 6.45% CoreMark cycle saving still lost to validity-only squashing at 52.313 MHz.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.79%): Balanced masked-OR ALU/EX selection with `syn_keep` cut post-multiply LUT depth from seven to five, but routing left selection delay near 6 ns and median Fmax fell from 52.313 to 50.326 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-2\n…[4276 of 7348 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0fafea859b9075fd]…\ns six radix-32 digits preserves t8 divide acceptance while MEM product forwarding confines MUL bubbles to immediate dependencies.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r12s0 (regression, +7.38%): Using radix-32 `subtract_ok` thermometer bits directly in a balanced threshold mux tree removes one-hot decode and payload masking, raising probe median Fmax from 83.245 to 89.392 MHz with 122 fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r13s1 (regression, +2.47%): Registering `PC+imm` in ID/EX removes the direct-target adder from EX recovery/alignment without adding a pipeline stage, but the resulting 2.47% throughput gain is insufficient for the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r13s2 (regression, -2.74%): Separate normal/recovery PC banks retain late `redirect` on the recovery bank's write enable and add a mux before every active-PC consumer, so registering the recovery selector alone does not isolate branch resolution or guarantee a timing gain at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
