{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n x=json.loads(line)\n if x['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-003-r8s2','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1']:\n  print(x['id'],x.get('critical_path'))\n  h=x['hypothesis']\n  print(h['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r8s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.is_auipc_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/mul_uu_63_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.653, 'arrival_ns': 18.727, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id']}\nInsert a four-entry fall-through instruction FIFO inside if_stage.sv,\nseparating speculative fetch acceptance from ID instruction acceptance.\nThis is one structural change: make the existing front end elastic.\nKeep single issue, nret=1, the current 16-entry agree predictor, early\nJAL, integer/multiply arithmetic and the t8 radix-16 divider unchanged.\n\nEach FIFO entry carries the fetched instruction, its own PC and the\npredicted_taken bit used to choose that fetch's successor. The fetch\nPC addresses the next unbuffered instruction; it is no longer also the\ndecode PC. On an accepted instruction-bus response, advance this PC\nusing the existing predictor. Capture responses only with imemReady\nasserted and available queue capacity. Holding the address while full\nmust not enqueue duplicate responses. Prediction and training retain\ntheir existing algorithms, reset state and EX-advancement qualification.\n\nWhen empty, expose the current raw response directly to ID. If ID can\nconsume it, advance fetch with no enqueue and no added issue latency.\nIf ID is held and the response is ready, enqueue it and advance fetch.\nWhen nonempty, expose only the oldest saved entry and assert output\nvalid independently of imemReady. Pop exactly when ID accepts that\nentry; concurrently enqueue a newly accepted response when space is\navailable. Use four explicit flop entries with a fixed head at index\nzero, shifting retained entries toward the head only on consumption.\nA simultaneous push writes index old_count - pop, with push taking\npriority over shift at that entry. Force syn_ramstyle=\"registers\";\navoid a circular-array read-pointer mux on the decode path. Capacity\nand acceptance logic must have no combinational ready/valid loop.\nA full queue may conservatively resume filling on the following cycle\na\n…[8330 of 11402 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2509004f33166ee9]…\non passed all 30 existing pipeline tests\nbut fell to 73.778 MHz in placement 0. That probe is not the proposed change;\nkeep the registered trap controls intact and specialize only fwd_w_en.\nOther probes of registered recovery and altered predictor state also lost\nfirst-placement Fmax, so none of those changes belongs in this hypothesis.\n\nNo measured fitness improvement is claimed. At 4203513 marker-delimited\ncycles, acceptance above 181.38 * 1.046 = 189.72348 iterations/second needs\nmedian Gowin Fmax above 79.750512 MHz. Check the full paths to confirm removal\nof branch-condition logic from forwarding and run all three placements;\nreject unless measured Fmax times iterations/cycle improves over the current\nchampion by more than 4.6 percent. Preserve nret=1 and all ports, decoder\nlegality, every memory and retirement invariant, the ten-cycle branch-to-divide\nprogress bound, and real/ALTOPS M arithmetic. Run unchanged formal, pipeline\nand decoder tests, cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART validation.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
