{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r11s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r11s0',\n 'title':'Move divider launch arithmetic into a narrow first-byte iteration',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 191.27 iterations/s at 76.107 MHz with 3978988 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Recent operation-decode and operand-routing probes repeatedly expose the path from signed normalization through the four serial launch-prefix steps into remainder_q. Prior micro_opt lookup-prefix duplication gained only 0.17 percent, and registered ALU decode regressed. Rebalance the existing divider's local arithmetic: remove prefix arithmetic from operand acceptance, then process the leading byte with two narrow digit selectors in the first existing busy cycle.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Keep the divider's request/result interface, seven busy cycles and earliest t8 result handshake unchanged. On accepted launch capture the full dividend magnitude into quotient_q and zero into remainder_q, along with the existing normalized divisor, widened registered multiples, signs, original dividend and exceptional-case flags. Remove the combinational four-bit restoring launch prefix and its small-divisor selection. There is no new pipeline stage or changed issue/retirement schedule.\n\nOn the first busy edge (iteration_q==0), consume eight leading dividend bits using two serial, narrowly sized radix-16 digit selectors. The first selector divides quotient_q[31:28] by the registered divisor. For each k=1..15, subtract multiple_q[k][3:0] from that nibble using a five-bit unsigned difference. A candidate succeeds only when multiple_q[k][35:4]==0 AND the subtraction's borrow bit is clear. Never truncate a large multiple without this high-bit-zero guard. Form mutually exclusive selects s0=\"'!g1, sk=gk&&!g(k+1), s15=g15. Use masked candidates and a balanced OR tree to obtain the four-bit remainder R4 and quotient digit Q4. Candidate zero returns the unchanged input nibble and quotient zero.\n\nForm the second unsigned trial T8={R4,quotient_q[27:24]}. Evaluate fifteen nine-bit differences against multiple_q[k][7:0], accepting only candidates with multiple_q[k][35:8]==0 and no borrow. Apply the same mutually exclusive selection to obtain eight-bit remainder R8 and four-bit second quotient digit Qlo. At this first busy edge capture quotient_q={old_quotient_q[23:0],Q4,Qlo} and remainder_q={24'\"'b0,R8}. Both selectors consume only state registered at launch; neither includes live signed-mode decoding or magnitude conversion. Use explicit unsigned widths and static generate loops. Do not implement a runtime division operator, ROM, priority chain, or full-width subtraction in these narrow selectors.\n\nFor iteration_q values 1..6, use the existing single full-width radix-16 recurrence exactly unchanged. The first busy cycle consumes eight bits, and the next six consume four each: 8+6*4=32. Completion still becomes sticky after t7 and can first transfer at t8. Preserve the existing real and ALTOPS controller and ALTOPS formulas exactly; only the real arithmetic's initial state and first busy update change. Reset cancellation, held completion, operand snapshots and the zero-divisor override remain authoritative. For a positive divisor, the first selected remainder is below D and fits four bits; the second trial is at most 255, so its selected remainder fits eight bits. This establishes the existing R<D recurrence invariant. Preserve signed quotient/remainder correction and INT_MIN/-1 semantics.\n\nThis is a single local divider datapath rebalancing micro-optimization. Preserve all ordinary ALU and multiply logic, direct M operands, forwarding, branch resolution, prediction, FIFO, memory handshakes, decoder legality, nret=1 and all RVFI contracts. Do not combine it with the rejected AUIPC, counter-update, x0-mask, operand-register or shared-shifter probes. Those independent exploratory variants all regressed and are not proposed for implementation.\n\nThe expected benefit is eliminating normalization followed by serial restoring steps on the launch edge while using small subtractors to absorb the displaced work after a register boundary. The two narrow selections, registered-multiple fanout and first-iteration state mux can become critical, and multiple-generation or ordinary EX feedback can cap the gain. Predict six percent higher fitness with low confidence pending vendor measurement; this estimate is not a measured improvement. Acceptance requires strictly more than 200.06842 iterations/s, approximately 79.607 MHz median Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n\nRetain all arithmetic, protocol, retirement and progress assertions. Update only internal-state expectations in divider tests: after launch Q=abs(A), R=0; first busy update consumes eight bits; each remaining update consumes four. Check each resulting state against an independent integer quotient/remainder model, retaining seven busy edges, t8 acceptance, live-input isolation, backpressure and reset checks. Cover small D values, leading-byte boundaries, large divisors with low-byte aliases, every sign combination, zero divisors and overflow. Run unchanged ALTOPS, pipeline, formal, RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation under both stall streams. Expect exactly 3978988 timed cycles. Normal ALTOPS formal does not prove the changed real divider arithmetic; retain real tests and the formal-deep obligation. Judge all three Gowin placements, not an isolated path or source-level depth.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Initialize the real divider with the full dividend magnitude and zero remainder. Replace launch-prefix arithmetic with two guarded, narrow radix-16 selectors on the first busy edge; keep the next six full-width iterations and exact existing completion protocol. Preserve all other arithmetic and ALTOPS behavior.'},\n {'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'Retain every result, exception and handshake check. Update the independent per-iteration model for zero launch work, eight first-cycle bits and six four-bit steps. Add first-byte thresholds, high-bit guards, signed boundaries, snapshots, result holds and reset coverage.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Replaced and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
