   \"logic_cells\": 38,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r11s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r11s0',\n 'title':'Isolate the divider launch nibble from full-width dividend negation',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 191.27 iterations/s at 76.107 MHz with 3978988 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Recent micro_opt operation-decode changes regressed, and timing repeatedly exposed magnitude preparation followed by the four-bit launch prefix. Prior lookup-prefix duplication gained only 0.17 percent. Compute only the leading dividend magnitude nibble with a small exact Boolean network, leaving the existing prefix arithmetic, divisor normalization and divider schedule intact.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Replace the dependency of the real divider's launch-prefix input on a full-width dividend negation with a dedicated four-bit magnitude calculation. Let n=signed_op && m_a[31] and z=OR(m_a[27:0]). Compute prefix[0]=m_a[28] XOR (n AND z). For k=1..3 compute prefix[k]=m_a[28+k] XOR (n AND (z OR OR(m_a[27+k:28]))). Use constant slices and a shared balanced lower-28-bit OR reduction. This is the exact identity (-x)[i]=x[i] XOR OR(x[i-1:0]) when negating, specialized to bits 31:28. It handles the carry boundary when all lower 28 bits are zero, including zero and INT_MIN, without propagating a carry from bit zero into the launch nibble.\n\nFeed this prefix directly to prefix_quotient[0] and to the large-divisor remainder initialization currently using dividend_magnitude[31:28]. Retain ordinary conditional negation for the lower 28 dividend bits used by quotient_q initialization; it can be expressed at 28-bit width and concatenated with prefix, using the original n derived from bit 31, never bit 27. This is bit-for-bit equivalent to the original full magnitude. Leave divisor_magnitude, its small-divisor guard, all four serial narrow restoring-prefix steps and every registered divisor multiple exactly unchanged. Do not replace the whole dividend or divisor with a full-width prefix network, duplicate signed/unsigned prefix engines, or change the prefix into a lookup table.\n\nThis is one local combinational arithmetic micro-optimization. Preserve the first four quotient bits computed at accepted launch, seven following radix-16 busy cycles, registered completion, earliest t8 result handshake, sticky backpressure, reset cancellation, captured signs, original dividend, zero-divisor override and INT_MIN/-1 results. Leave result sign correction, ordinary ALU and multiply expressions, direct M inputs, all ALTOPS formulas and schedule, forwarding, holds, prediction/FIFO, memory handshakes, decoder legality, nret=1 and every RVFI contract unchanged. No new state, stage, instruction latency, synthesis constraints or keep attributes are required.\n\nThe intended gain is removing a wide carry path from the leading-nibble input while adding only one shared reduction and four conditional XOR functions. Prior ordinary carry-select and registered-decode changes did not isolate this input. A broader exploratory probe replacing both complete magnitudes with prefix-OR networks passed the existing arithmetic and pipeline tests but regressed median Fmax to 65.408 MHz. That result motivates limiting the network to these four dividend bits; it does not establish a gain for this narrower version. Other exploratory AUIPC, counter, forwarding, operand-register, shifter and first-byte-iteration variants also regressed and are excluded. Predict six percent higher fitness with low confidence pending timing of the exact narrow implementation. Divisor normalization, the remaining restoring prefix, ordinary EX feedback and routing may still limit Fmax.\n\nRetain all existing arithmetic, exception, protocol and progress assertions. Add directed post-launch quotient/remainder and final DIV/REM checks for all 16 high nibbles combined with lower-28-bit values zero, one, all ones and a single one at each position. Cover signed and unsigned operations, positive/negative small divisors 1..17, large divisors with low-nibble aliases, divisor zero, INT_MIN/-1 and deterministic random operands. Compare against independent Python integer magnitudes and quotient/remainder results, retaining exact original launch state, seven busy edges, t8 acceptance, live-input isolation, backpressure and reset checks. Run unchanged ALTOPS, pipeline, formal and full RVFI cosimulation gates plus canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation under both bus-stall streams. ALTOPS formal does not prove the changed real arithmetic; retain real coverage and the formal-deep obligation.\n\nExpect exactly 3978988 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. Judge the median of all three Gowin placements times measured iterations/cycle. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 200.06842 iterations/s, approximately 79.607 MHz at unchanged cycles. No measured improvement is claimed.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Compute only the leading four dividend magnitude bits from a shared lower-28-bit OR reduction and four conditional XORs. Use this nibble for both launch-prefix input and the large-divisor remainder path; retain native lower-28-bit negation and every divisor, prefix, recurrence, result and ALTOPS behavior.'},\n {'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'Retain all existing assertions and exact divider timing. Add high-nibble/lower-bit carry-boundary vectors, signs, large-divisor aliases and independent post-launch plus final arithmetic comparisons.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r11s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert isinstance(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'],int)\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
