{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The divider mux tree is the strongest probe: median Fmax is 89.392 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles, implying a 7.39% fitness gain. It also uses 122 fewer LUTs. I’m updating the hypothesis file to this single `micro_opt` change and finishing the recurrence check."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r12s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r12s0\ntitle: \\\"Select radix-32 divider results with a balanced threshold mux tree\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion delivers 208.94 iterations/s at 83.245 MHz, with 3984075\n  measured CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md records that r5s1 improved timing\n  with parallel divisor subtraction and that r11s1 isolated normalization\n  and multiplication at existing register boundaries. The current radix-32\n  recurrence still converts 31 borrow decisions into adjacent-threshold\n  one-hot selects, gates 32 full candidate payloads, then OR-reduces them.\n  This adds decode and distribution after the wide subtraction decisions.\n  Prior micro_opt carry-select, ALU decode and launch-prefix attempts\n  r9s0/r10s0/r11s0 did not improve these recurrence selection paths.\n  Exploratory timing identifies divider recurrence paths very close to\n  ordinary EX feedback. Rewriting only the local selection topology can\n  reduce this logic and its routing burden without changing cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the real radix-32 divider's one-hot candidate masking and\n  balanced OR reduction with a balanced tree of ordinary two-input muxes.\n  Preserve all 31 independent unsigned 38-bit subtractions, their full\n  borrow bits, registered 37-bit multiples, and the exact radix and\n  request/preparation/iteration/completion schedule. Do not change any\n  ordinary ALU operation, multiplier, pipeline stage, or external port.\n\n  Let T be the existing 37-bit digit_trial and g[k]=\"'!difference[k][37]\n  for k=1..31. For positive D these decisions form a thermometer code:\n  g[k] is true exactly when k <= floor(T/D). Because the legal recurrence\n  maintains R<D, T<32D and the winning digit is in 0..31. Construct\n  unmasked 37-bit leaves C[0]={T[31:0],5'\"'d0} and\n  C[k]={difference[k][31:0],k[4:0]} for k=1..31. Remainder candidates\n  may be truncated only after retaining the original full-width borrow\n  decision; the selected nonnegative remainder fits 32 bits.\n\n  Build the following statically generated mux levels, with all indices\n  elaboration-time constants. A true decision always selects the upper\n  candidate or subtree:\n    L1[j] = g[2*j+1]  ? C[2*j+1]  : C[2*j],  j=0..15;\n    L2[j] = g[4*j+2]  ? L1[2*j+1] : L1[2*j], j=0..7;\n    L3[j] = g[8*j+4]  ? L2[2*j+1] : L2[2*j], j=0..3;\n    L4[j] = g[16*j+8] ? L3[2*j+1] : L3[2*j], j=0..1;\n    selected_digit = g[16] ? L4[1] : L4[0].\n  Each node asks whether the winning digit reaches the lower boundary\n  of its upper half. Thus every path chooses exactly the same largest\n  successful subtraction as the current one-hot network. The select\n  decisions already exist independently: do not introduce a serial\n  priority encoder, a new subtract-after-selection stage, or a global\n  binary digit decoder feeding a variable-index array. Keep quotient\n  digit and remainder together in the same payload. No keep/preserve\n  attributes or added state are needed. Existing intermediate names\n  masked_digit and digit_or16/8/4/2 may be retained while changing their\n  assignments to the above unmasked leaves and mux levels; document\n  their new role. Remove the now-unused digit_select one-hot vector.\n\n  Preserve t0 magnitude capture, t1 widened-multiple/two-bit-prefix\n  preparation, six five-bit recurrence edges t2..t7, sticky completion,\n  and earliest result acceptance at t8. Preserve operand isolation,\n  reset cancellation, older-memory overlap, backpressure and result\n  stability. For D=0 all g bits are true and the tree selects digit 31,\n  matching the old internal selection; the existing captured zero-divisor\n  override remains authoritative for architectural results. Keep signed\n  truncation, remainder sign, INT_MIN/-1 and all RV32M corner cases.\n  Leave RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and controller timing untouched.\n  Preserve nret=1, decoder legality, RVFI order, memory handshakes and all\n  evaluation invariants. This is one combinational selection optimization,\n  with no change to architectural scheduling, memory or prediction.\n\n  A temporary implementation of exactly this mux-tree change passed\n  Verilator lint, all eight full RVFI cosim comparisons for selftest and\n  random1/random2/random3 with ready buses and with both stall streams,\n  and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. CoreMark still\n  takes 3984075 measured cycles. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 report\n  86.773/89.392/89.392 MHz: median 89.392 MHz, implying 224.3733\n  iterations/s, 7.39 percent above the 208.94 champion. The probe uses\n  6491 LUTs versus 6613, with unchanged 2795 registers, zero LUT-RAM,\n  four BSRAMs and two DSPs. The median-placement worst reported arrival\n  is 16.481 ns in ordinary EX-to-ID feedback. These are exploratory\n  measurements, not an accepted evaluation or a formal proof. Placement\n  variation and ordinary EX paths remain the principal risks.\n\n  Retain every existing real-arithmetic and protocol test vector. Adapt\n  the recurrence test's representation-specific digit_select assertion\n  to require subtract_ok == (1 << expected_digit)-1, while retaining\n  exact integer-divmod checks of selected_digit, every widened multiple,\n  all differences and final results. Exercise all 32 digits, kD-1/kD/kD+1\n  thresholds, wide T, divisors around powers of two and 0xffffffff, signs,\n  zero, overflow, changing live operands, held results and reset. The\n  selected_digit reference must remain independent Python arithmetic,\n  not a restatement of the mux tree. Run the unchanged pipeline and\n  ALTOPS suites, normal formal, and real-arithmetic checks; ALTOPS alone\n  does not prove the modified divider arithmetic. Retain the formal-deep\n  obligation for real M arithmetic. Formal has not been run on the probe.\n  During implementation repeat all unchanged correctness and CoreMark\n  gates and all three Gowin placement options. Acceptance requires\n  measured fitness strictly above 218.55124 iterations/s, or 87.072453\n  MHz at unchanged cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the radix-32 one-hot masked-candidate OR network with the\n      explicit five-level threshold-controlled mux tree over unmasked\n      remainder/digit candidates. Remove digit_select; retain the full\n      subtract/borrow arithmetic, multiples, all state and timing, real\n      exceptional cases, ALTOPS branch and every other ALU operation.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all arithmetic vectors, full-width difference/multiple\n      checks, independent divmod comparisons, exceptional-case and exact\n      handshake timing assertions. Replace the removed internal one-hot\n      signal check with the equivalent expected thermometer-code check\n      on subtract_ok; retain all radix-32 digit and threshold coverage.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r12s0.yaml');h=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r12s0' and h['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
