{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r4s0': print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\nrg -n 'u_alu|alu_result' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncat cores/bench/generated/r13s0-threshold-tests.log\"","aggregated_output":"Replace the combinational ALU result-selection chain and its adjacent\nEX jump/divide override muxes with mutually exclusive result masks and\nexplicitly balanced bitwise-OR reductions. Keep every arithmetic\noperator and pipeline boundary unchanged. This is one local selection\ntopology optimization; it must add no instruction cycles or state.\n\nIn alu.sv compute integer, shift, compare, and multiply candidate values\nindependently. Decode the five-bit op into exact equality selects for\nthe supported combinational operations. Mask each 32-bit candidate with\nits replicated select and combine candidates in small balanced groups,\nfollowed by a balanced OR of those groups. Avoid a priority if/else\nchain, a running OR accumulator, or a serial ternary chain. Unsupported\nop values, including DIV/REM on the combinational output, still produce\nzero. Keep the three existing multiply expressions and their signed\nwidths unchanged; the target is selection after the arithmetic, not a\nchange to multiplication or division algorithms. Under\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS use the exact existing substitute expressions as\ncandidates under the same operation masks.\n\nIn ex_stage.sv similarly form disjoint selectors for the normal ALU\nresult, PC+4 jump link, and completed divide result. Preserve the\nexisting priority exactly: divide first, then jump, then normal ALU.\nUse masked candidates and a balanced OR into reg_q.alu_result, keeping\nreset, stall retention, execute_wait bubbles, and all other EX fields\nunchanged. Do not move redirect into payload enables or gate arithmetic\nwith retirement validity. Branch resolution, misalignment traps,\nforwarding, retained WB availability, divider request/completion\nhandshakes, and the nret=1 interface retain their current behavior.\n\nThe expected gain is 12 percent in fitness from Fmax alone, with the\nsame marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. This is initially an unmeasured\nprediction. Gowin may already optimize some case arms in parallel,\nmay reassociate an OR tree unfavorably, or may expose divider feedback\nas the next limit. Confirm the actual reduction in post-arithmetic\nLUT depth with vendor reports; do not rely on source-code appearance.\nAcceptance requires fitness strictly above 121.82762, more than\n4.6 percent above 116.47, using median vendor Fmax and measured cycles.\n\nPreserve every existing check and all decoder legality rules. Exercise\nall combinational ALU operations and unassigned op values with exact\ninteger references, including signed/high-half multiply boundaries and\nall shift amounts. Run the existing real and ALTOPS arithmetic suites,\npipeline tests, formal and cosimulation gates, and canonical CoreMark\nCRC/UART/marker checks under both bus stalls. Check that cycle counts\nand division latency remain unchanged and that jump-link and divide\nresults still forward correctly through stalled dependent consumers.\n\n...                                                                      [100%]\n3 passed in 109.91s (0:01:49)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
