{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l); h=d.get('hypothesis',{}); s=h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n if any(w in s.lower() for w in ['comparator','comparison','carry-select','one-hot']):\n  print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' '+d['title'])\n  for p in s.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n   if any(w in p.lower() for w in ['comparator','comparison','carry-select','forwarding topology','boolean']): print(p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nhyp-20261002-001-r1s0 Share DIV and REM through a fused-subtract unsigned divider\nImplement the divider as 32 statically unrolled, progressively sized stages.\nStarting with R_0 = 0, stage k = 1..32 forms the k-bit trial remainder\nT_k = {R_(k-1), magnitude_a[32-k]}. Compute a single (k+1)-bit unsigned\ndifference D_k = {1'b0, T_k} - {1'b0, magnitude_b[k-1:0]}. Accept it only\nwhen all divisor bits above k-1 are zero and D_k[k] is zero. The accepted\ndecision is quotient[32-k]; R_k is D_k[k-1:0] if accepted, otherwise T_k.\nAt k=32 the upper-divisor-zero condition is constant true. Use static\ngenerate widths, with an explicit first stage, and share the upper-bit\nnonzero reductions. Derive the comparison directly from the subtraction's\nborrow bit; do not infer a separate comparator followed by a subtractor.\nThis keeps early stages narrow and makes quotient/remainder sharing explicit.\n\nhyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-branch prediction with a 64-entry bimodal table\nCarry the exact predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the actual successor using the existing\nforwarded branch comparison, JAL/JALR target calculation, and trap\nfallback to PC+4. Redirect and squash the younger instruction only when\nthat successor differs from the saved prediction. Use the resolved\nsuccessor for EX/MEM.pc_next and RVFI regardless of prediction. Correct\npredictions must neither flush nor insert a bubble, and a predicted-taken\nbranch that resolves not-taken must recover to its own PC+4.\n\nhyp-20261002-001-r2s0 Halve serial divider depth with parallel radix-4 digit selection\nFor each of two statically generated digit steps, let R be the current\n32-bit remainder and Q the 32-bit combined dividend/quotient shift\nregister. Form the unsigned 34-bit trial T = {R, Q[31:30]}. In parallel\ncompute three 35-bit differences Xk = {1'b0,T} - {1'b0,k*D}, for\nk = 1, 2, 3. Let gk = !Xk[34], using the subtraction's borrow bit\ndirectly rather than a separate comparison followed by subtraction.\nSelect the largest \n…[47939 of 51011 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:af9919b64276b948]…\nmoving recovery-payload reset reached only 90.185 MHz and is excluded. The final proposal instead retains reset and writes the correction bank only for real redirects, removing the selector-dependent enable term and speculative idle writes; its final three-placement timing is pending. It passes the existing pipeline/fetch suites, canonical CoreMark at exactly 3984075 timed cycles, and an inductive active-PC recurrence equivalence proof over arbitrary control inputs and full-width correction addresses. Full-core riscv-formal and RVFI cosimulation have not been run in this hypothesis session and remain required implementation gates. Confidence is low because the extra PC mux, bank flops and routing can offset the benefit, and divider recurrence/EX forwarding become competing limits. Separate exploratory two-bit Boolean comparison, delayed-training, unconditional-bank-capture and word-address-bank variants reached 79.927, 86.966, 84.505 and 79.298 MHz median respectively; all are excluded from the final change.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
