{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r6s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Bypass EX operand muxes for RV32M arithmetic\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 152.61 iterations/s at median 68.545 MHz, with\n  4491485 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md records that\n  registered forwarded operands and the parallel radix-16 divider\n  moved the bottleneck to ordinary EX arithmetic and result selection.\n  The current EX still feeds every arithmetic unit through the AUIPC\n  PC-versus-rs1 and immediate-versus-rs2 operand muxes. Every legal\n  RV32M operation uses rs1 and rs2, so those muxes are unnecessary on\n  multiplier and divider paths. Their delay and shared fanout affect\n  both multiplication and divider launch. The r4s0 micro_opt result\n  warns against another global result-mux rewrite; specialize the\n  arithmetic inputs instead, retaining the existing execution timing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give the existing multiplier and divider direct connections to the\n  fully forwarded, registered ID/EX source operands. This is one local\n  operand-routing optimization: remove the generic integer operand\n  selection from every RV32M arithmetic path without adding a register,\n  changing an arithmetic algorithm, or changing instruction latency.\n\n  In alu.sv add two internal-module 32-bit inputs named m_a and m_b.\n  In ex_stage.sv connect them directly to rs1 and rs2, which already\n  alias in.rs1_val and in.rs2_val. These are complete operands captured\n  after forwarding, not unforwarded register-file outputs. Keep the\n  current a and b ports connected to alu_a and alu_b for RV32I integer\n  operations, including AUIPC, immediate arithmetic and memory address\n  generation. Do not insert another mux or validity gating on m_a/m_b.\n\n  Change the operand references in all three existing real multiply\n  expressions to m_a and m_b, retaining their exact signedness, widths,\n  high/low selection and combinational behavior. Route the real divider's\n  signed-magnitude preparation, original-dividend snapshot, sign flags\n  and zero-divisor detection from m_a/m_b as well. Consequently the\n  prefix and divisor-multiple bank are launched from these direct\n  operands. Leave the prefix arithmetic, parallel radix-16 recurrence,\n  multiple generation, result correction and all state machines intact.\n  Preserve seven busy cycles, registered completion, earliest t8 result\n  consumption, sticky completion, reset cancellation and operand capture.\n\n  In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, apply each existing multiply and divide/rem\n  add/subtract/XOR expression to m_a/m_b, with every opcode, constant\n  and request/completion timing unchanged. The real and formal branches\n  must use the same architectural operands. RV32I output expressions\n  continue using a/b. Keep unsupported combinational operations zero.\n  Preserve the EX divide snapshots, result selection, forwarding, bus\n  holds, redirects, decoder legality, traps and exact-once retirement.\n  The core's nret=1 interface is unchanged; these added ports belong\n  only to its internal ALU module. Do not change the multiplier count,\n  divider topology, memory address path, pipeline boundaries or controls.\n\n  A temporary probe implementing only this routing change used the\n  unchanged Gowin evaluation wrapper and placement options 0/1/2.\n  Fmax was 72.277/72.222/72.222 MHz, median 72.222 MHz. Option 0 used\n  4723 LUTs, 1900 flip-flops, four BSRAMs and six DSPs, compared with\n  the champion's 4654 LUTs, 1901 flip-flops, four BSRAMs and six DSPs.\n  The existing real-arithmetic and ALTOPS ALU suites and pipeline suite\n  passed with their ALU drivers adapted to the new internal inputs.\n  All eight full RVFI trace comparisons passed: selftest and random1/2/3,\n  each with ready buses and with both instruction and data stalls.\n  The canonical stalled CoreMark binary passed the unchanged CRC/UART\n  validator, bounded-access check and start/stop marker checks. Markers\n  remained 15801 and 4507286, exactly 4491485 measured cycles. Thus\n  measured iterations/cycle and median Fmax imply fitness 160.80,\n  approximately 5.36 percent above 152.61. This is exploratory evidence,\n  not an accepted evaluation. Formal and formal-deep were not run on\n  the probe; implementation must still pass all unchanged gates.\n\n  Predict 5 percent higher fitness with medium confidence. The timing\n  margin over the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold is small, and changed\n  placement or an exposed forwarding/result path could consume it.\n  Retain this exact local scope rather than combining unrelated changes.\n  Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 159.63006; at unchanged\n  cycles the required median Fmax is approximately 71.70 MHz. Measure\n  every placement option and actual marker cycles in the final evaluation.\n\n  Retain all existing tests. Adapt real and ALTOPS ALU input drivers\n  to drive the new operands, and add isolation cases with a/b deliberately\n  different from m_a/m_b. Check that every M operation uses m_a/m_b and\n  integer operations still use a/b. Cover signed and unsigned multiply\n  halves, DIV/REM sign combinations, zero divisors, INT_MIN/-1, changing\n  both input pairs after divide acceptance and held results. Run the\n  existing pipeline dependency/stall tests, formal and RVFI cosimulation\n  gates and canonical CoreMark validation. Preserve real-arithmetic\n  checks and the formal-deep obligation; ALTOPS alone does not establish\n  arithmetic correctness. No verification contract is relaxed.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add m_a/m_b inputs and use them exclusively for the unchanged real\n      multiplier expressions and divider launch operands, including\n      magnitudes, original dividend, sign flags and zero-divisor detection.\n      Use the same operands in every existing M-extension ALTOPS formula.\n      Keep a/b for RV32I arithmetic and preserve all arithmetic widths,\n      corner cases, divider state, handshakes and instruction latencies.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect ALU m_a/m_b directly to the existing fully forwarded ID/EX\n      rs1/rs2 values, bypassing the PC and immediate operand muxes for M\n      operations. Keep the existing alu_a/alu_b connections for integer\n      arithmetic and leave EX controls, result selection, snapshots,\n      forwarding, redirects and EX/MEM register behavior unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt the standalone ALU drivers to initialize and drive m_a/m_b\n      while retaining every arithmetic and handshake check. Add independent\n      operand-pair tests with exact multiply/divide references, signedness\n      boundaries and divide corner cases. Verify integer operations use\n      a/b, M operations use m_a/m_b, and accepted divide operands remain\n      isolated from subsequent changes to either input pair.\n  - file: test/test_alu_altops.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the new M operand inputs in the existing ALTOPS suite without\n      changing reference formulas or constants. Add distinct a/b versus\n      m_a/m_b cases for multiply and captured DIV/REM substitutions, and\n      retain exact completion timing, operand isolation and reset checks.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r6s0' and d['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50 <= d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <=50\nfor c in d['changes']:\n assert (Path('cores/bench')/c['file']).is_file()\nprint('Schema and target paths valid:',p)\nPY\ngit status --short\ngit diff --stat\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
