{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_24", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n x=json.loads(line)\n if x['id'].endswith('r9s1'):\n  print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:5200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replace the launch-prefix plus seven radix-16 iterations with launch\nnormalization plus seven uniform radix-32 iterations. This is one\nstructural redesign of the divider's digit width and register boundaries.\nRetain the existing request/busy/done controller, seven busy edges,\nearliest t8 result handshake and sticky completion. Keep the integer\npipeline, combinational multiplication, forwarding, four-entry elastic\nfetch queue, predictor and all memory/retirement interfaces unchanged.\n\nOn accepted launch capture the original dividend, signedness, quotient\nand remainder signs, desired result kind, zero-divisor flag and normalized\nunsigned divisor. Initialize a 35-bit quotient register to the 32-bit\nunsigned dividend magnitude with three leading zero bits; initialize the\n32-bit remainder to zero. Remove small_divisor qualification and every\nprefix quotient/remainder calculation. Preserve full-width normalization,\nincluding the magnitude of INT_MIN, and request-time operand snapshots.\n\nCapture widened divisor multiples 1D through 31D at launch in a full\nregistered bank. Use 37 unsigned bits throughout: 31*UINT_MAX fits and\nall five incoming bits participate in each borrow decision. Reuse the\nnormalized divisor register for 1D. Generate each constant product from\nat most three signed power-of-two terms with explicitly widened shifts,\nadds and subtracts, registering the full product independently. Use a\ncanonical signed-digit expansion: repeatedly choose digit 2-(k modulo 4)\nfor an odd k, subtract that digit, then shift k right; this yields terms\n0,+1,-1. Form the positive terms first, then subtract negative terms.\nExamples are 3D=4D-D, 11D=16D-D-4D, 13D=16D+D-4D,\n19D=16D+4D-D, 21D=16D+4D+D, 23D=32D-D-8D,\n25D=32D+D-8D, 27D=32D-D-4D, 29D=32D-D-2D and 31D=32D-D.\nKeep all intermediates 37 bits, so the exact arithmetic is retained.\nDo not explicitly replace the even-product registers with shifts of odd\nregisters: that area-saving probe regressed to 68.083 MHz. Do not use\ncarry-save constant-product logic: that probe regress\n…[2129 of 5201 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:becdf58e1f57cbf8]…\nmately 3.2 percent higher fitness, below\nacceptance. The carry-save, odd-sharing and alternative additive bank probes\npassed the adapted real/ALTOPS arithmetic suites. The additive bank\nreached only 76.446 MHz; retain the specified canonical formulation.\nFormal has not been run.\n\nPredict three percent higher fitness with low confidence. This is the\nbest measured structural direction in this proposal session, but it has\nnot demonstrated the required tournament margin. The wider bank and\n32-way selection increase routing/fanout, and ordinary EX feedback now\ncompetes with divider launch/recurrence for the critical path. Acceptance\nrequires strictly more than 200.06842 iterations/s, or 79.606984 MHz median\nFmax at unchanged cycles. Measure all three placements and the actual\nmarker-delimited cycles. Preserve nret=1, core ports, every hard invariant\nand all evaluation files. Run unchanged formal and RVFI cosimulation gates;\nALTOPS formal alone does not establish the new real arithmetic.\n\nAdapt internal arithmetic\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
