{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_84", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Separate address generation and register complete memory requests\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion delivers 208.94 iterations/s at 83.245 MHz with 3984075\n  timed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts at ID/EX.pc[24]\n  and reaches data memory through EX/ALU result selection. The shared\n  alu_result currently carries arithmetic, AUIPC, jump links and division\n  results as well as memory addresses, while MEM formats store data and\n  derives byte masks and alignment. Prior structural successes isolated\n  operand and M-unit register boundaries; r10s1's control predecode did\n  not remove arithmetic paths. Split address generation from ordinary\n  register arithmetic and terminate memory-request preparation at EX/MEM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give EX a dedicated address/request lane beside its integer ALU. This\n  is one structural partition of execution and its EX/MEM payload, with\n  no additional pipeline stage, cycle, speculation or memory buffering.\n\n  Add mem_address[31:0], mem_mask[3:0] and mem_misaligned to ex_mem_t.\n  Preserve the existing write_data field but change its payload from raw\n  rs2 to the formatted bus write data. Keep rs1_val and rs2_val separately\n  for exact architectural/RVFI source reporting, including divide snapshots.\n\n  Compute memory_address = in.rs1_val + in.imm directly from registered,\n  fully forwarded operands with full 32-bit wraparound. Do not use the\n  generic ALU's AUIPC/register or immediate/register operand selection,\n  its operation mux, or its divide/jump result mux for this address.\n  Prepare memory_write_data and memory_mask in parallel: for mem_width=0,\n  replicate rs2[7:0] four times and use 0001 << memory_address[1:0]; for\n  width=1, replicate rs2[15:0] twice and use 0011 << address[1:0]; otherwise\n  preserve rs2 and use 1111. Prepare an unqualified alignment flag from\n  (width==2 && address[1:0]\"'!=0) || (width==1 && address[0]). All shifts and\n  masks have the original four-bit semantics. Do not truncate the address.\n\n  Capture this complete payload under the existing EX/MEM reset, hold,\n  divide-wait bubble and transfer rules. A downstream dmem stall freezes\n  the entire older request. During divide waits, older entries drain once\n  and EX/MEM.valid becomes zero; retained payload must have no effects.\n  Preparation need not be gated by instruction validity or redirect.\n  Do not add bus-ready gating to the arithmetic or payload input paths.\n\n  In MEM, drive dmem_addr directly from mem_address and wdata_rep directly\n  from the already-formatted write_data. Use mem_mask directly for both\n  load and store masks. Qualify mem_misaligned with the existing valid\n  memory-operation predicate before applying the existing trap and\n  reg_write suppression. Use mem_address for load-byte extraction and\n  aligned RVFI addresses. Keep current request-valid gating, load\n  extension, MEM forwarding, product selection and MEM/WB retirement.\n  A trapped or invalid entry must never issue memory effects. Preserve\n  the current exact-once acceptance behavior through arbitrarily long\n  bus stalls and the original full-width address range contract.\n\n  Complete the address/arithmetic split by routing AUIPC through the\n  existing PC-relative branch_target = in.pc + in.imm adder. Set the\n  ordinary ALU'\"'s a input directly to rs1, retaining its existing b input\n  selection. The EX forward/result mux priority becomes completed divide,\n  jump PC+4, AUIPC branch_target, then ordinary alu_result. This removes\n  the PC-source mux ahead of general ALU arithmetic without adding any\n  AUIPC cycle. Preserve JAL/JALR link values, all target calculations,\n  alignment traps, branch comparisons, recovery and predictor training.\n  No predictor policy or fetch-queue change is part of this proposal.\n\n  Keep the current forwarding/interlock priorities, single retirement,\n  registered multiplication, t8 division schedule, real M arithmetic and\n  all ALTOPS formulas. Preserve nret=1, core ports, default-illegal\n  decoding, raw RVFI operands and every correctness/evaluation invariant.\n  Keep the native adders; do not introduce carry-select rewrites, keep\n  attributes, synthesis constraints or modifications to contract files.\n\n  A temporary implementation of this exact partition passed Verilator\n  lint, the complete existing pipeline suite, and all eight full RVFI\n  comparisons for selftest/random1/random2/random3 with ready buses and\n  both stall streams. Canonical CoreMark passed CRC, UART success-text,\n  bounded-access and marker validation in exactly 3984075 measured cycles.\n  Gowin placements 0/1/2 measured 87.053/87.155/87.155 MHz, using 6592\n  LUTs, 2801 registers, zero LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs. The\n  87.155 MHz median implies 218.758432 iterations/s, a 4.699 percent gain.\n  These are exploratory measurements; formal has not been run on the\n  probe. The new median-placement worst path is divider recurrence at\n  16.768 ns, with ordinary forwarding and fetch occupancy paths nearby.\n\n  The address-only probe reached 84.563 MHz median; adding AUIPC separation\n  reached 85.945 MHz. Preparing the complete memory request at EX/MEM\n  produced the measured 87.155 MHz result. These trials motivate the\n  full partition rather than merely adding a second address register.\n  The gain narrowly exceeds acceptance, so confidence is low: placement\n  sensitivity and the remaining divider/forwarding paths may erase it.\n  Require all three placements and unchanged marker-delimited throughput;\n  acceptance is strictly above 218.55124 iterations/s, or 87.072453 MHz\n  at 3984075 cycles. Predict five percent higher fitness, not a guaranteed\n  accepted improvement. Repeat all unchanged correctness gates.\n\n  Retain every existing test and add directed coverage of prepared\n  requests: every load/store width and byte lane, signed loads, negative\n  offsets, misalignment suppression, x0 bases and full-width wraparound\n  arithmetic. Check load/MUL/DIV/ALU-produced bases and store operands,\n  long dmem holds overlapping division, reset and wrong-path stores.\n  Check positive/negative AUIPC immediates, immediate dependent consumers,\n  subsequent loads/stores and ordinary PC-relative control transfers.\n  Preserve exact addresses/data, source snapshots, retirement ordering,\n  cycle schedules and exact-once memory effects. Update packed-layout\n  helpers only if needed; do not weaken assertions or remove vectors.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Add mem_address, mem_mask and mem_misaligned to ex_mem_t. Document write_data as formatted bus data while retaining raw architectural rs2_val, all result/product fields and nret=1.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Prepare direct rs1+imm addresses, replicated store data, byte masks and alignment flags and capture them at the existing EX/MEM boundary. Feed ALU.a from rs1 and select the existing PC-relative sum for AUIPC after the ALU, preserving divide/jump priority, all stage holds, bubbles, snapshots and control-flow behavior.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Consume registered memory-request address, formatted data, mask and alignment flag. Use mem_address for load extraction and RVFI aligned addresses. Retain validity-qualified misalignment traps, request enables, memory backpressure, load/product forwarding and exact-once retirement.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Preserve existing architectural and timing assertions; add prepared-request and AUIPC dependency tests covering widths, lanes, offsets, full-width address arithmetic, produce