{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml')\ns='''id: hyp-20261002-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Separate sequential and recovery PC register banks without extra bubbles\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 224.83 iterations/s at 89.575 MHz and 3984075\n  measured CoreMark cycles. Its recorded worst path runs from registered\n  EX rs2 operands through branch resolution and fetch to the PC register,\n  with 38 cells and 17.799 ns arrival time. Structural retiming of forwarded\n  operands, M execution and complete memory requests produced earlier gains.\n  Predictor predecode attempts r10s2 and r11s2 retained the combinational\n  recovery boundary and regressed. A delayed conditional recovery probe\n  in r10s2 also regressed, and an extra recovery bubble risks the existing\n  branch-to-divide progress bound. Split the PC state so the recovery\n  decision is registered without delaying the target fetch.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace IF's single PC register, whose input mux combines ordinary next\n  fetch and late EX recovery, with separate sequential and recovery PC\n  registers plus a registered one-cycle recovery selector. This is one\n  structural change to fetch-PC state and the recovery boundary. Preserve\n  all instruction-stage boundaries, prediction choices, recovery latency,\n  forwarding, memory-request preparation and M-unit schedules.\n\n  In if_stage.sv introduce sequential_pc_q, recovery_pc_q and\n  recovery_valid_q. Define the logical current PC as\n  pc = recovery_valid_q ? recovery_pc_q : sequential_pc_q.\n  Use this selected PC consistently for io_imemAddr, raw_fetch.pc,\n  PC+4, PC-relative branch/JAL targets and the agree-table lookup index.\n  The selected PC is the instruction address even when the physical\n  sequential bank still contains a discarded wrong-path address.\n\n  Each non-reset edge sets recovery_valid_q to the existing redirect\n  input and captures redirect_target into recovery_pc_q unconditionally.\n  Reset clears the selector and initializes both PC banks to RESET_PC.\n  Unconditional target-payload capture keeps the late branch predicate\n  out of a 32-bit bank write-enable network; its only new destination is\n  the one-bit recovery selector. Existing redirect fanout to queue\n  occupancy and ID/EX validity remains unchanged.\n\n  Update sequential_pc_q with the existing predicted next_pc whenever\n  fetch_accept is true. If fetch_accept is false while recovery_valid_q\n  is true, copy recovery_pc_q into sequential_pc_q. Otherwise hold it.\n  There is deliberately no combinational redirect term in this bank's\n  input or enable. On a recovery edge it may advance a discarded fetch;\n  the newly registered selector makes the target bank authoritative\n  immediately after that same edge. The original immediate queue flush\n  and ID annul make the target available through the existing empty\n  bypass on the usual next cycle, with no additional recovery bubble.\n\n  The sequential-bank fallback when the target is unavailable is\n  essential: when recovery_valid_q clears on the next edge, the target\n  must remain the selected address if imemReady was low. On successful\n  target fetch, next_pc is computed from that target's actual instruction\n  and pre-edge predictor state, so execution continues at its sequential\n  or predicted successor. Preserve the general simultaneous-redirect\n  priority: a new registered recovery overrides any sequential-bank\n  update. Queue reset/flush, capacity, push/pop, fixed head and empty\n  bypass retain their exact original equations and cycle behavior.\n\n  Keep EX redirect_target, control_advance, predictor training, branch\n  comparisons, jump links, alignment traps and architectural pc_next\n  unchanged. Preserve FENCE.I serialization and redirect priority over\n  unavailable imem. A held EX branch recovers only when it advances;\n  reset cancels a pending recovery selection. Keep payload preparation\n  independent of redirect and use existing validity-only squashing.\n  Do not add a delayed ID annul or delay predictor training. No stale\n  queue entry, invalid divide, wrong-path store or duplicate retirement\n  may gain an effect from the temporary sequential-bank contents.\n\n  This moves the wide recovery-versus-next-PC mux to the output of\n  registered PC banks. The comparator feeds a one-bit register rather\n  than the sequential PC's 32-bit input selection; target arithmetic\n  separately ends at the recovery payload register. The new output mux\n  adds delay to instruction-memory addressing and predicted next-PC\n  feedback, so improved median Fmax is a hypothesis, not a guarantee.\n  Divider recurrence and ordinary forwarding may still limit timing.\n\n  Expect identical marker-delimited CoreMark cycles to the champion.\n  Predict seven percent higher fitness with low confidence until vendor\n  measurements are available. Acceptance requires more than 235.17218\n  iterations/s, or approximately 93.695 MHz at 3984075 measured cycles.\n  Require all three Gowin placements and all unchanged correctness gates,\n  including formal, real M arithmetic, full RVFI cosimulation with both\n  bus-stall streams and canonical CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation.\n  Preserve nret=1, all external ports, default-illegal decoding, ALTOPS\n  formulas and timing, every hard invariant and all contract files.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the single next-PC/recovery input mux with sequential and\n      unconditionally captured recovery PC banks selected by registered\n      redirect. Feed all fetch calculations from the selected logical PC.\n      Preserve an unavailable target in the sequential bank when the\n      selector clears, immediate queue flushing, prediction/training and\n      all existing fetch handshakes and cycle latency.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Retain the existing predictor and queue conservation model. Add\n      directed checks for recovery with empty/full queues, target stalls\n      of arbitrary duration, target branches/JAL, sequential-bank fallback,\n      consecutive redirects, reset during selection and full-width PC\n      arithmetic. Assert identical visible addresses and delivered bundles\n      to the original single-PC state machine on every cycle.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve every existing architectural and cycle assertion. Exercise\n      taken/not-taken misses, JALR links, misaligned control transfers,\n      FENCE.I, held branches and branch-to-divide progress with target-side\n      imem stalls and overlapping dmem holds. Check exact-once retirement\n      and training, correct source values and PCs, and no wrong-path effects.\n'''\np.write_text(s)\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(s),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid; acceptance Fmax:',224.83*1.046*3984075/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
