{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +657.59%): Moving combinational ALU `/` and `%` into a shared four-bit-per-cycle, eight-cycle divider removes full-division depth from the EX timing path, letting clock-rate gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.25%): A 64-entry BHT plus early JAL cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45% at nearly unchanged Fmax (5.514 MHz), but lost to iterative division because prediction left the combinational DIV/REM timing bottleneck intact.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +11.77%): A narrow four-bit launch prefix lets quotient_q/remainder_q isolate the final restoring steps from sign c\n…[5621 of 8693 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b7b44f3e2b55226f]…\ns six radix-32 digits preserves t8 divide acceptance while MEM product forwarding confines MUL bubbles to immediate dependencies.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r12s0 (regression, +7.38%): Using radix-32 `subtract_ok` thermometer bits directly in a balanced threshold mux tree removes one-hot decode and payload masking, raising probe median Fmax from 83.245 to 89.392 MHz with 122 fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r13s1 (regression, +2.47%): Registering `PC+imm` in ID/EX removes the direct-target adder from EX recovery/alignment without adding a pipeline stage, but the resulting 2.47% throughput gain is insufficient for the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r13s2 (regression, -2.74%): Separate normal/recovery PC banks retain late `redirect` on the recovery bank's write enable and add a mux before every active-PC consumer, so registering the recovery selector alone does not isolate branch resolution or guarantee a timing gain at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
