{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r14s1\ntitle: \\\"Separate register-file reset state from distributed data storage\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current register file resets 992 writable data bits and forces flop\n  storage. Keeping data in asynchronous distributed RAM while resetting\n  only per-register initialization tags could reduce storage and routing\n  pressure without adding a pipeline stage or changing instruction timing.\n  Earlier operand retiming required preventing BSRAM inference; explicitly\n  selecting distributed RAM preserves asynchronous reads and the separate\n  ID/EX operand-capture boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the resettable flop register-file array with a 32x32 array marked\n  syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed\\\", plus a 32-bit initialized_q bitmap. Reset\n  only the bitmap. Write the data array on \"'!reset && w_en && w_addr!=0;\n  set the corresponding initialized bit on each architectural nonzero\n  write. Preserve synchronous writes and both asynchronous read ports.\n\n  For each read, preserve the exact existing priority: address zero yields\n  zero; otherwise a matching current WB write yields w_data; otherwise\n  return the stored word if initialized_q[address] is set, or zero if it\n  is clear. Preserve the current same-cycle bypass behavior even during\n  reset; reset has priority over actual writes at the clock edge. Unknown\n  or stale uninitialized RAM contents must never reach a read result.\n  Repeated reset invalidates every old word without clearing the data RAM.\n\n  Keep the module interface, ID forwarding, fully resolved operand capture,\n  all hazards, predictor, fetch FIFO, memory requests, execution latency\n  and retirement unchanged. This is one structural storage/reset split,\n  with no cache, register renaming, synchronous-read conversion, additional\n  pipeline stage or speculative architectural write. Do not weaken tests\n  that require every register to read as zero after reset. Keep x0 writes\n  suppressed and retain every source/result RVFI assertion.\n\n  Require Gowin'\"'s area report to confirm distributed RAM storage and no\n  additional BSRAM. Inspect timing to ensure the ID/EX operand registers\n  remain an independent boundary. If the tool ignores the attribute,\n  record that fact rather than claiming a distributed-memory result.\n  Fmax improvement is a hypothesis, not a measured result at this point:\n  the new bitmap lookup and zero mask can lengthen the read path, and\n  divider recurrence or EX forwarding may still limit timing.\n\n  Expect exactly 3984075 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. Measure all\n  three Gowin placements and the unchanged stalled CoreMark run. Fitness\n  must exceed 235.17218 iterations/s to clear acceptance, approximately\n  93.695 MHz at that cycle count. Preserve nret=1, every core port,\n  default-illegal decoding, real and ALTOPS M arithmetic, memory bounds,\n  canonical CRCs, UART success text, retirement order and all contract\n  files. Run unchanged formal, unit tests and full RVFI cosimulation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: >-\n      Replace resettable flop data storage with explicitly asynchronous\n      distributed RAM and a resettable initialization bitmap. Preserve\n      x0, both combinational read ports, synchronous writes, WB write-first\n      priority and architectural zero values after every reset.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: >-\n      Retain all existing vectors. Add write-all/reset/read-all sequences,\n      untouched-register reads, x0 writes, same-cycle WB bypass on an\n      uninitialized register, and repeated reset with write enable high.\n      Check every read port and preserve the existing bypass priority.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: >-\n      Preserve existing pipeline and cycle assertions. Exercise WB writes\n      seen by both ID sources, EX/MEM forwarding over older RF values,\n      read-before-first-write after reset, reset after nonzero register\n      contents, and backend holds. Check exact RVFI operands/results,\n      source priority, retirement order and memory side effects.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r14s1.yaml'\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(open(p)),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
