{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r14s1\ntitle: \\\"Register the final architectural writeback value at MEM/WB\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  MEM already selects the complete architectural producer value for ID\n  forwarding, but MEM/WB separately captures execute_result and load_data\n  and WB repeats the selection after the register. Move that selection\n  before MEM/WB so the register-file write data and WB write-first bypass\n  start from one registered final value. This removes duplicated payload\n  storage and late selection without adding instruction latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime final writeback selection across the existing MEM/WB boundary.\n  In mem_stage, capture fwd_data into reg_q.alu_result instead of capturing\n  execute_result there. fwd_data already equals load_data for mem_to_reg\n  and execute_result otherwise, including the registered multiply result\n  and completed divide result. In wb_stage, drive w_data directly from\n  in.alu_result. Document that this existing field now carries the final\n  architectural result for every instruction, including loads.\n\n  Preserve mem_wb_t's layout for this hypothesis, including read_data;\n  synthesis can prune its now-unused writeback-only storage. Preserve\n  reg_q.read_data's assignment for internal compatibility. Do not remove\n  raw mem_rdata or any other RVFI field. Leave MEM-to-ID forwarding and\n  execute_result generation exactly unchanged. No result mux or arithmetic\n  is moved onto the EX feedback path.\n\n  Capture the final value under exactly the existing MEM/WB reset, hold\n  and normal-transfer rules. A dmem hold clears only retirement validity\n  and retains all payload bits; a load's value becomes architecturally\n  visible only when its memory operation advances. Preserve bypass_valid,\n  wb_w_en, destination tags, x0 suppression, trap-adjusted write eligibility\n  and the register file's WB write-first behavior. Never write or retire a\n  held instruction twice. Keep raw source snapshots, aligned memory masks,\n  bus data, resolved successors and retirement order unchanged.\n\n  This is one structural retiming of the writeback result boundary. Keep\n  pipeline depth, dependency bubbles, memory handshakes, branch recovery,\n  prediction, fetch buffering, divider timing and multiplication unchanged.\n  Preserve nret=1, all external ports, default-illegal decoding, real and\n  ALTOPS arithmetic and every hard invariant. Modify no contract file.\n\n  The predicted benefit is eliminating a 32-bit WB mux and approximately\n  32 live result-storage bits from the timed circuit, and shortening WB\n  writes and the WB-to-ID bypass. The same final MEM result already feeds\n  ID operand capture, so selecting it for MEM/WB adds a register load to\n  an existing network. That added fanout and the unchanged divider/EX\n  paths can erase the benefit; do not infer acceptance from RTL structure.\n  This prediction is pending vendor timing, with low confidence.\n\n  Expect unchanged 3984075 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. Require all\n  three Gowin placements, canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker\n  validation, existing unit assertions, full RVFI cosimulation and the\n  unchanged formal gate. Acceptance requires strictly more than 235.17218\n  iterations/s, approximately 93.695 MHz at the expected cycle count.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: >-\n      Capture the already-selected fwd_data as MEM/WB.alu_result, preserving\n      reset, payload retention on dmem holds, normal capture, raw memory\n      RVFI fields and the existing MEM forwarding network.\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: >-\n      Drive w_data directly from the registered final value in alu_result.\n      