{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"agent_message","text":"The head-and-tail prototype passes the existing fetch conservation and pipeline tests. It preserves the four-entry capacity and empty bypass; the tail’s read mux feeds the head register rather than decode. Vendor timing is still running."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r15s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-002-r15s1\ntitle: \\\"Separate the fetch queue decode head from a circular tail\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The four-entry fall-through fetch queue gained 5.45 percent by overlapping\n  fetch with backend holds. Its current shift-on-pop implementation moves\n  up to three complete instruction bundles on each consumption and computes\n  a variable insertion index from count minus pop. Vendor timing exposes\n  paths from occupancy through decode/hazard control back into queue data\n  registers. Prior circular queues put a pointer-selected read on the decode\n  path, while removing empty bypass added latency. A dedicated head register\n  with a circular tail can avoid both costs while reducing tail movement.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the four-entry shifting instruction FIFO with one dedicated decode\n  head and a three-entry circular tail. This is one structural change to\n  instruction buffering. Keep total capacity four, empty raw-response\n  bypass, the existing predictor and the exact fetch/issue timing. Decode\n  reads only the head register when nonempty. The tail's pointer-selected\n  read feeds the head register's D input, never the combinational decode\n  output, source-address comparisons or predictor lookup.\n\n  Retain count_q and the existing definitions of fetch_accept, pop and push:\n  fetch_accept = imem_ready && count_q \"'!= 4; pop = count_q != 0 && !stall;\n  push = fetch_accept && (count_q != 0 || stall). Preserve the existing count\n  update and PC advance. In particular, a full queue does not accept a raw\n  response on its pop cycle; it resumes fetching on the next cycle. Do not\n  introduce a decode-ready dependency into fetch capacity.\n\n  Store complete if_id_t bundles in head_q and three flop-backed tail entries.\n  For a push into an empty queue, capture raw_fetch into head_q. On pop with\n  old count greater than one, refill the head from tail_q[tail_read_q]. On\n  pop with old count equal to one and fetch_accept asserted, replace the\n  head directly with raw_fetch. Otherwise retain the head payload. Stale\n  payload is harmless when occupancy is zero and output bypass selects raw.\n\n  Define tail_pop = pop && count_q > 1 and tail_push = push &&\n  (count_q > 1 || (count_q == 1 && !pop)). Write raw_fetch only to the tail'\"'s\n  current write slot when tail_push is true. Advance the corresponding\n  two-bit pointer modulo three on each tail transfer: 0 to 1 to 2 to 0.\n  Both pointers reset to zero on reset or redirect, together with count_q.\n  Circular reads have old-state semantics on simultaneous operations; at\n  count four, raw acceptance is inhibited, so a full-tail pop never races a\n  same-slot push. At count one, simultaneous pop/push replaces the head and\n  moves neither tail pointer. At count zero, consumed raw bypass data is\n  not also enqueued. Preserve these conservation rules across all stalls.\n\n  Remove the shifted queue array, count-minus-pop insertion arithmetic and\n  the associated per-entry shift/insert arbitration. Keep the tail explicitly\n  mapped to registers, as in the current queue, and keep its dynamic read\n  behind the head register. Payload writes need no reset/redirect gating;\n  flushed occupancy and reset pointers invalidate all discarded entries.\n  Preserve every instruction's PC and prediction metadata as one bundle.\n\n  Keep execution, forwarding, load/MUL interlocks, division, memory handshakes,\n  predictor training and recovery, FENCE.I, trap rules and RVFI retirement\n  unchanged. Preserve nret=1, all core ports, all hard invariants and every\n  protected file. Do not combine this with AUIPC retiming or other datapath\n  changes. The expected benefit is fewer wide queue updates and less late\n  insertion control; tail read muxing and pointer logic are the main risks.\n  Expect unchanged CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires more than 235.17218\n  iterations/s, or median Fmax above 93.694361 MHz at 3984075 cycles.\n\n  Validate queue conservation against the existing randomized model and\n  preserve every existing pipeline cycle assertion. Run full RVFI cosim\n  with ready and stalled buses, the unchanged formal gate, canonical stalled\n  CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks and all three vendor placements.\n  Lint and the existing fetch/pipeline suites pass on a temporary prototype;\n  vendor timing and complete fitness remain to be assessed.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the four shifting queue entries with a dedicated head register\n      and three-entry circular flop tail using separate modulo-three read\n      and write pointers. Implement the specified head refill, direct\n      replacement and tail transfers. Retain count, empty bypass, capacity,\n      predictor, PC advance and flush timing exactly; remove push_index and\n      shifting of retained tail entries.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve the existing cycle-by-cycle queue/predictor conservation\n      model and coverage. Add long simultaneous-transfer runs wrapping both\n      tail pointers repeatedly, fill/drain at every pointer position, head\n      replacement at count one and reset/redirect from every occupancy and\n      pointer position, including ready-low responses and repeated PCs.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing architectural and timing assertions. Exercise tail\n      wraps across load/MUL dependencies, divide waits, long dmem holds,\n      prediction recovery and FENCE.I, checking PC/instruction/prediction\n      association, exact-once stores, retirement order and no stale entry\n      after flush or reset. No execution latency expectation changes.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
