{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('cores/bench/generated/bypass_probe');r=build_all('.',p/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'final_timing');s=summarize(r);(p/'final_timing.json').write_text(json.dumps({'summary':s,'options':r},indent=2));print(json.dumps(s,indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 57.217,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    56.747,\n    57.217,\n    57.217\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      56.747,\n      26\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      57.217,\n      27\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      57.217,\n      27\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 3556,\n  \"ff\": 1562,\n  \"lutram\": 0,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 6,\n  \"logic_levels\": 26,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_31_s1\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_2_s1\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -12.622,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 22.916,\n    \"logic_cells\": 128,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg --files cores/bench/test\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r4s1.yaml')\ns='''id: hyp-20261002-002-r4s1\ntitle: \\\"Resolve forwarding before the ID/EX operand registers\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 116.47 at 52.313 MHz median Fmax and 4491485\n  marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md and the structural entries\n  in log.jsonl show that divider isolation and validity-only squashing\n  removed earlier timing bottlenecks. A fresh make timing TARGET=bench\n  reproduced its placement-option-0 result of 48.369 MHz. The detailed\n  25.969 ns path starts at register-file BSRAM output, passes through ID\n  data selection, several EX forwarding/operand muxes, the signed DSP\n  multiplier, and result selection before reaching the data-memory input.\n  The multiplier is only part of this path; operand selection precedes\n  every execute operation, including divider normalization and launch.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move operand forwarding across the existing ID/EX register boundary.\n  Resolve each incoming instruction's register values in ID, capturing\n  fully forwarded source operands into ID/EX. EX then consumes those\n  registered values directly, without the existing EX/MEM-versus-MEM/WB\n  forwarding muxes ahead of arithmetic. This is one structural retiming\n  of operand delivery, with no added pipeline stage or instruction cycle.\n  Preserve the champion's validity-only squash and payload-hold rules.\n\n  For each raw ID source address, choose the youngest eligible producer:\n  first the instruction currently executing in EX, then the instruction\n  currently in MEM, then the register-file value with its existing WB\n  write-first bypass. Suppress x0 producers and force x0 reads to zero.\n  Compare against the corresponding current-stage destination tags, not\n  the old EX-forwarding stage ages. EX supplies its current combinational\n  architectural write value: ordinary ALU result, PC+4 for JAL/JALR, or\n  the completed divider result. A load in EX is unavailable and must\n  never forward its address as data. MEM supplies its aligned, extracted\n  and sign/zero-extended load value for loads, or its registered ALU\n  result otherwise. Both producer enables use valid and the stage's\n  trap-adjusted reg_write, so misaligned jumps or loads cannot inject a\n  write that architectural writeback suppresses.\n\n  Retain the existing conservative one-cycle load-use interlock. When\n  the immediately preceding load is in EX, hold the dependent fetch and\n  inject the existing ID/EX bubble. On the next eligible capture, that\n  load is in MEM and its actual load value enters the consumer's operand\n  register. Older dmem backpressure prevents capture until the bus is\n  ready. An outstanding divide also holds ID/EX; on its existing t8\n  completion transfer, its result can simultaneously supply the next\n  instruction's ID capture. Do not add a separate load or divide bypass\n  delay. WB write-first remains the oldest forwarding source.\n\n  Once ID/EX contains a valid instruction, its captured source operands\n  must already be complete and must remain unchanged through all backend\n  holds. This removes reliance on keeping a late forwarding source alive\n  while that consumer stalls. Keep divider operand snapshots and its\n  single-launch/result-retention protocol unchanged. Preserve the current\n  MEM/WB retention and retirement behavior; removing EX forwarding must\n  not replay older stores, writes or retirement. Use the captured source\n  values for EX comparisons, addresses, store data and RVFI. Repeated\n  redirects by a held branch and validity-only wrong-path squashing keep\n  their existing behavior. No path from redirect may enter raw source\n  addressing, the operand-data muxes or ID payload enables.\n\n  Make the register-file implementation compatible with an explicit\n  operand-capture boundary: retain the existing two asynchronous reads,\n  synchronous single write, reset-to-zero and write-first semantics, but\n  use flop storage rather than allowing Gowin to absorb the read/capture\n  network into BSRAM. A supported syn_ramstyle = \\\"registers\\\" attribute on\n  the array achieved this in the probe; an equivalent explicit register\n  bank is acceptable. This storage mapping is part of the operand-delivery\n  retiming, not a new architectural latency. It trades roughly 930 extra\n  flip-flops and 830 LUTs for lower launch delay and a clean register\n  boundary. Keep ordinary portable RTL behavior in simulation and formal.\n  The unqualified RAM-inference version of the retimed probe stopped in\n  Gowin inference without producing reports, so do not depend on that\n  mapping. Do not introduce vendor primitives or change tool settings.\n\n  Export narrow result/producer interfaces from EX and MEM to ID, and\n  relocate the forwarding selection logic accordingly. The EX result tap\n  must depend only on registered ID/EX state, not the incoming ID operands;\n  the MEM tap depends on EX/MEM and memory data. This keeps the feedback\n  path synchronous and avoids combinational loops. EX must not retain\n  active late operand forwarding in addition to the new early network.\n  Leave ALU arithmetic, multiplier implementation, divider prefix and\n  seven iterations, ALTOPS formulas, decode rules, pipeline retirement\n  width and the exact top-level port contract unchanged.\n\n  An isolated RTL probe passed the canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART\n  checks with exactly 4491485 measured cycles. With explicit flop-based\n  register-file storage, its initial placement-option-0 timing reached\n  57.089 MHz, versus 48.369 MHz for the unchanged champion on that option;\n  its longest path moved inside the divider recurrence. This is promising\n  probe evidence, not a completed formal proof or an accepted result.\n  A multiplier-only split was also probed, but exposed other operand and\n  divider paths and did not improve timing, supporting the broader shared\n  operand-delivery target rather than changing multiply latency.\n\n  Predict 10 percent higher fitness with medium confidence. The remaining\n  divider recurrence and the new EX-to-ID or MEM-to-ID capture paths may\n  limit the gain, and the larger register bank can increase routing cost.\n  Acceptance requires fitness above 121.82762; at unchanged CoreMark\n  cycles this means median Fmax above approximately 54.719398 MHz.\n  Check all three Gowin placement options and actual marker cycles.\n  Preserve nret=1, all hard invariants and all evaluation files. Validate\n  bypass age priority, trap-suppressed producers, loads, divides, held\n  operands, redirects and bus stalls using directed unit tests plus the\n  unchanged formal/cosimulation gates and canonical CoreMark checks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire current EX and MEM architectural result/producer taps into ID\n      operand preparation. Connect forwarding comparisons to raw incoming\n      ID source tags and the corresponding current-stage producer tags.\n      Preserve WB write-first, existing hazards, pipeline validity and\n      channel-0-only retirement. Remove active late EX operand forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Retarget the two-producer priority network to ID operand capture:\n      current EX overrides current MEM, with register-file/WB as fallback.\n      Respect stage validity, trap-adjusted reg_write, x0, and unavailable\n      EX loads. Keep producer selection independent of branch redirect.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the fully resolved register-source values at the existing\n      ID/EX boundary, retaining payload hold and valid-only squash rules.\n      Ensure backend holds preserve complete operands and load-use keeps\n      its current bubble timing. Document the earlier forwarding boundary.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume captured ID/EX sources directly for all execution and RVFI\n      paths. Export the current architectural result and valid,\n      trap-adjusted producer eligibility to ID; exclude EX loads and use\n      PC+4 for jumps and completed results for divides. Preserve divider\n      control, alignment traps, branch timing and EX/MEM hold behavior.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export the existing load-extraction result or registered ALU value,\n      plus valid trap-adjusted write eligibility, for early operand capture.\n      Preserve byte lanes, memory handshakes, misalignment suppression,\n      retained WB payload and exact-once retirement unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Use explicit flop storage, or the demonstrated Gowin registers\n      inference attribute, to preserve an independent ID/EX operand\n      capture boundary. Keep both asynchronous read ports, synchronous\n      writes, complete reset semantics, x0 and WB write-first behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add youngest-writer priority tests with EX, MEM and WB targeting\n      the same source, both operand ports, x0 and rd/source overlap.\n      Exercise ALU/MUL/jump-link forwarding, load-use with byte/halfword\n      extension, divider completion to an immediate consumer, and stalled\n      branches/stores retaining captured values after producers drain.\n      Include simultaneous bus stalls, wrong-path instructions, invalid\n      producer payloads and misaligned jump/load producers that must not\n      forward. Check exact RVFI sources/results/PC/order and exact-once\n      memory effects. Retain all arithmetic and liveness checks.\n'''\np.write_text(s)\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nv=yaml.safe_load(s);jsonschema.validate(v,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')));print('Revised structural hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
