{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261002-001-r2s0':\n  print(json.dumps({k:d.get(k) for k in ['id','title','hypothesis','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path','implementation_notes']},indent=2))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261002-001-r2s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Halve serial divider depth with parallel radix-4 digit selection\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The champion reaches 91.82 iterations/s at 41.241 MHz, but its reported\\nworst path still starts at cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_5_s0 and passes\\nthrough the divider and EX to data memory: 29.998 ns and 131 cells.\\nEach iteration currently chains four restoring subtract/select steps,\\nfollowed on completion by sign correction and the EX result mux.\\nLESSONS.md establishes division depth as the dominant clock bottleneck.\\nThe prior micro_opt attempt, hyp-20261002-001-r1s0, reduced area but\\nregressed to 3.887 MHz by retaining a full combinational divider.\\nOptimize the champion's bounded iteration datapath instead: reducing\\nits serial arithmetic depth can improve every instruction's clock rate\\nwithout increasing division stalls or changing pipeline control.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the real iterative divider's four serial radix-2 steps\\nwith two serial radix-4 steps. Each radix-4 step produces two quotient\\nbits by evaluating subtraction candidates concurrently. Retain exactly\\nfour quotient bits per iteration, eight iteration cycles per request,\\nand the existing request, final-iteration bypass, and sticky completion\\nprotocol. This is a local arithmetic implementation change inside\\nalu.sv, with no new pipeline stage or execution scheduling policy.\\n\\nOn request acceptance, form the existing unsigned divisor magnitude D\\nand register D and 3*D. Form 2*D by wiring a left shift of registered D.\\nRepresent all multiples as unsigned 34-bit values: 3*(2^32-1) must not\\nwrap. Compute 3*D as the explicitly widened sum of D and 2*D on the\\nrequest path, never by an adder in the iteration feedback path. Retain\\nthe existing captured dividend, quotient magnitude, sign flags, and\\ndivide-by-zero flag. Reset the added state with the existing divider.\\n\\nFor each of two statically generated digit steps, let R be the current\\n32-bit remainder and Q the 32-bit combine\n…[3807 of 6879 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:62e8d26ec6962926]…\nepresentable k*D-1, k*D, and\\nk*D+1 boundaries for k=1,2,3, including divisors around 2^30,\\n2^31, and 2^32-1 where 2D or 3D needs more than 32 bits. Exercise\\nquotients with repeated radix-4 digits 0,1,2,3, mixed digits, all\\nsign combinations, zero divisors, and INT_MIN/-1. Use exact integer\\ntruncation-toward-zero references and seeded random operands.\\nPreserve assertions of unchanged eight-iteration completion and\\ntest direct consumption, held completion, reset, and back-to-back\\nrequests so arithmetic optimization cannot alter the protocol.\\n\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"outcome\": \"regression\",\n  \"fitness\": 102.27,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 45.934,\n  \"cycles\": 4491485,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_3_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_0_s100\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -16.811,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 27.105,\n    \"logic_cells\": 48,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_fwd\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\",\n      \"cpu/u_hazard\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
