{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"tail -14 cores/bench/generated/r7probe/divider_probe.log && sed -n '1,55p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r7s1.yaml && git status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "..                                                                       [100%]\n2 passed in 11.68s\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r7s1\ntitle: Pipeline M-unit launch and multiply delivery without global multiply stalls\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 161.18 iterations/s at 67.751 MHz and 4203513\n  marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at\n  ID/EX ctrl.is_auipc and ends in divider remainder initialization.\n  LESSONS.md and the structural log show that moving arithmetic across\n  register boundaries has helped, but r6s1's registered multiplier forced\n  an extra cycle on every MUL and raised CoreMark cycles by 2.09 percent.\n  That candidate reached 73.119 MHz but missed acceptance. Preserve its\n  necessary separation of divider launch preparation while placing the\n  multiply product in the existing EX/MEM pipeline instead of holding\n  all issue until multiplication completes. This targets both the timing\n  boundary and the unnecessary scheduling cost of that M-unit design.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure the M-unit launch/result boundaries: multiply progresses\n  through EX/MEM and supplies its result in MEM, while divider launch uses\n  its own direct-source narrow initialization path. This is one structural\n  M-unit redesign. It retains the existing instruction-stage count,\n  predictor, register-file mapping and nret=1. Do not introduce a global\n  multiply wait, scoreboard, out-of-order retirement or new memory state.\n\n  At the existing EX/MEM edge, capture a signed 66-bit product computed\n  from signed 33-bit operands derived directly from the fully forwarded\n  ID/EX rs1_val and rs2_val. Extend A with its sign only for MULH/MULHSU;\n  extend B with its sign only for MULH; zero-extend otherwise. Explicitly\n  cast both 33-bit operands signed before multiplying. Carry an is_multiply\n  bit and a high/low selector with the EX/MEM payload. Product capture,\n  metadata and instruction validity must use the same EX/MEM advancement,\n  bubble and downstream-hold rules. This permits one independent multiply\n  per cycle and introduces no separate request/completion controller.\n\n  In MEM, select product[31:0] for MUL or product[63:32] for MULH/MULHSU/\n  MULHU. This single extended product gives exact signed and unsigned\n  results without post-product subtraction. Feed the selected value to\n  both the MEM-to-ID forwarding tap and MEM/WB architectural-result field.\n  Remove active multiplication from the ordinary EX ALU result/mux path;\n  only the dedicated product register captures the raw multiply output.\n  Do not route an unregistered product through EX-to-ID forwarding.\n\n  Generalize the existing conservative EX-load dependency predicate to\n  include valid legal EX multiplies. An unavailable nonzero destination\n  matching either incoming raw source field holds fetch and inserts the\n  existing ID/EX bubble. Backend holds retain priority. Exclude EX\n  multiplies from the EX producer enable. A dependent instruction then\n  captures the complete MEM product on the next eligible cycle, using\n  existing MEM forwarding. Independent instructions, independent consecutive\n  multiplies and multiply-to-x0 need no multiply-induced bubble. Keep\n  EX-over-MEM-over-regfile/WB age priority, so a younger eligible writer\n  still overrides an older product. Keep divider execute_wait unchanged.\n\n  A held older memory instruction freezes EX/MEM product and metadata with\n  the rest of the payload. A divider wait may drain an older multiply\n  normally and then replace it with an invalid bubble. Multiplication\n M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/test/test_r7_probe.py\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
