{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_105", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r8s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Decouple fetch with a fixed-head elastic instruction queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion reaches 181.38 iterations/s at 76.245 MHz and\n  4203513 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. Its combinational IF/ID path\n  advances the fetch PC only when ID advances, so every load-use bubble,\n  divider wait and older data-memory stall wastes an opportunity to\n  capture an instruction from the independently stalled instruction bus.\n  Both buses are unavailable about 22 percent of cycles. Buffered fetch\n  can overlap those independent delays without changing execution width.\n\n  LESSONS.md and the structural entries in experiments/log.jsonl show\n  that moving forwarding before ID/EX and separating validity from\n  recovery helped, whereas the r6s1 and r7s1 multiplier pipelines exposed\n  other arithmetic limits and added cycles. The present proposal keeps\n  those proven operand boundaries and attacks the pipeline's lockstep\n  fetch/issue coupling. No prior structural entry tested fetch buffering.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a four-entry fall-through instruction FIFO inside if_stage.sv,\n  separating speculative fetch acceptance from ID instruction acceptance.\n  This is one structural change: make the existing front end elastic.\n  Keep single issue, nret=1, the current 16-entry agree predictor, early\n  JAL, integer/multiply arithmetic and the t8 radix-16 divider unchanged.\n\n  Each FIFO entry carries the fetched instruction, its own PC and the\n  predicted_taken bit used to choose that fetch's successor. The fetch\n  PC addresses the next unbuffered instruction; it is no longer also the\n  decode PC. On an accepted instruction-bus response, advance this PC\n  using the existing predictor. Capture responses only with imemReady\n  asserted and available queue capacity. Holding the address while full\n  must not enqueue duplicate responses. Prediction and training retain\n  their existing algorithms, reset state and EX-advancement qualification.\n\n  When empty, expose the current raw res\n…[7254 of 10326 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:47abad3ff802f4d2]…\nlly change u_hazard.imem_ready to if_id_w.valid; retain\n      io_imemReady on u_if.imem_ready and retain stall_if on u_if.stall.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Integrate instruction-fetch synchronization for legal FENCE.I:\n      on advancement after older memory completes, flush buffered younger\n      instructions and resume at its PC+4. Preserve branch recovery,\n      alignment traps, predictor training, arithmetic and divider timing.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed and randomized FIFO conservation and ordering checks,\n      empty bypass, all occupancies, simultaneous push/pop, fetch during\n      backend holds, issue during imem stalls, full queue and held-address\n      cases. Check redirect/reset flushes, prediction metadata, FENCE.I,\n      load/divide dependencies, memory effects, RVFI fields and order.\n      Retain all existing architectural correctness assertions and adapt\n      only scheduling assumptions changed by independent fetch acceptance.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
