{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predictor|return|gshare|BTB|BHT|agree|train_next' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Early direct-branch prediction with a 64-entry bimodal table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches PC+4 and flushes on every taken branch or\\njump. IF/ID is combinational, so each such redirect loses one potential\\ninstruction-acceptance cycle, not the two-cycle penalty suggested by\\nthe five-stage description. CoreMark's repeated loops and direct calls\\nshould benefit from predicting their successors before EX. The recorded\\n199.837 ns critical path runs through forwarding and the combinational\\nALU, leaving substantial apparent timing headroom for a small predictor;\\nthis proposal targets iterations/cycle rather than claiming a divider\\ntiming improvement. LESSONS.md is absent and log.jsonl contains only the\\nbaseline, with no previous predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches PC+4 and flushes on every taken branch or\\njump. IF/ID is combinational, so each such redirect loses one potential\\ninstruction-acceptance cycle, not the two-cycle penalty suggested by\\nthe five-stage description. CoreMark's repeated loops and direct calls\\nshould benefit from predicting their successors before EX. The recorded\\n199.837 ns critical path runs through forwarding and the combinational\\nALU, leaving substantial apparent timing headroom for a small predictor;\\nthis proposal targets iterations/cycle rather than claiming a divider\\ntiming improvement. LESSONS.md is absent and log.jsonl contains only the\\nbaseline, with no previous predictor experiments.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one early direct-control-flow predictor: a 64-entry, two-bit\\nsaturating direction table indexed by fetch PC[7:2], together w