{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r13s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r13s2',\n'title':'Separate speculative and recovery PC registers at predictor recovery',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 224.83 iterations/s, and its reported worst path runs from ID/EX.rs2_val through branch recovery to the fetch PC. Better direction accuracy alone has limited headroom: the prior architectural profile contains 529588 conditional branches, approximately 55124 agree-predictor misses, and only 13735 returns. Earlier comparison/control predecodes and training-destination predecode did not clear acceptance. Separate the late recovery decision from wide PC capture instead of changing prediction capacity.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the single fetch-PC register with two candidate address registers and one registered selector. normal_pc_q holds the normal predictor-selected successor; recovery_pc_q holds the EX correction address. The active PC is recovering_q ? recovery_pc_q : normal_pc_q, and drives both imem_addr and every existing fetch-PC use, including table indexing, B/J target formation and raw_fetch.pc. Capture normal_pc_q on the existing fetch_accept, independent of redirect. Capture recovery_pc_q when \"'!recovering_q || redirect, so the address is prepared independently of whether the normal path is chosen and remains stable throughout an instruction-bus hold after recovery. Set recovering_q on redirect, otherwise clear it on fetch_accept, otherwise retain it. Reset both address registers to RESET_PC and the selector to zero. Redirect retains priority over fetch acceptance and stalls. This representation must exactly reproduce the original PC sequence, including simultaneous acceptance and redirect, prolonged bus stalls, queue-full cycles, and successive control transfers.\n\nThe predictor integration change moves normal-versus-recovery selection to a mux after candidate registers. The late branch decision principally determines a one-bit state transition rather than selecting between two different wide PC data cones before one register. The guarded recovery-address capture may still receive redirect as an enable; inspect the resulting critical path rather than assuming the feedback disappears. Do not add a recovery cycle, change training cadence, retime operand comparison, or combine this with a counter representation change.\n\nKeep the 16-entry agree table, PC[5:2] indexing, weakly-agree reset, saturating training, pre-edge table semantics, saved predicted_taken, early JAL and sequential JALR. Preserve the four-entry fixed-head FIFO and bypass, its redirect flush, all raw payload and ID operand enables, branch resolution, alignment traps, FENCE.I serialization, and architectural pc_next. Preserve nret=1, external ports, decoder legality, both real and ALTOPS arithmetic, all memory/retirement invariants and all contract files.\n\nExpect exactly 3984075 timed CoreMark cycles. The timing benefit is unmeasured and low confidence: a wide mux after the PC registers can lengthen the instruction-memory path, added flops may worsen routing, and other paths may limit Fmax. An exploratory Boolean two-bit comparison tree passed an all-input combinational equivalence proof, pipeline/fetch tests and canonical CoreMark at the original cycle count, but reached only 79.927 MHz median, so it is excluded. A one-cycle predictor-training pipeline reached only 86.966 MHz median at 3984039 cycles and is also excluded. Earlier registered-recovery probes inserted latency; this proposal keeps the original recovery cycle and separates only address storage.\n\nRequire unchanged formal and RVFI cosimulation, predictor/FIFO tests, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks under both stall streams, and all three Gowin placements. Verify PC equivalence against the original recurrence and that the complete instruction/prediction bundle uses the selected active PC. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 235.17218 iterations/s, approximately 93.695 MHz at unchanged cycles. No measured fitness improvement is claimed.'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Represent fetch PC with normal and recovery address registers plus a registered selector. Preserve the original externally observed PC transition on every cycle and use the selected PC consistently for prediction, bus address and instruction metadata. Preserve table, queue and reset/recovery priorities.'},\n{'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Retain all predictor and queue assertions. Compare selected PC each cycle with the original reset/redirect/fetch_accept recurrence under arbitrary stalls, full queue, redirects and concurrent training. Exercise prolonged holds immediately after redirect, release with empty bypass and adjacent control transfers.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain architectural, progress and cycle assertions. Cover predicted and mispredicted control transfers, held branches, unavailable instruction memory at recovery, FENCE.I, misaligned targets and wrong-path memory instructions. Check exact retirement PCs, successors, order, operands and links.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
