{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r13s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\ns=d['hypothesis']\na=s.index('Capture normal_pc_q')\nb=s.index('Reset both address registers',a)\ns=s[:a]+'''On every non-reset clock, unconditionally capture normal_pc_q <= fetch_accept ? next_pc : pc, recovery_pc_q <= redirect_target, and recovering_q <= redirect. The current selector is a one-cycle record of the preceding redirect, not a sticky pending flag. If fetch is stalled after a redirect, normal_pc_q captures the selected recovery PC before recovering_q clears; the external PC therefore remains held. If a new redirect arrives during that hold, its newly captured address wins through the selector. Neither wide register has a live redirect-controlled enable or data input. '''+s[b:]\ns=s.replace('The guarded recovery-address capture may still receive redirect as an enable; inspect the resulting critical path rather than assuming the feedback disappears.', 'Only the one-bit selector directly consumes the live redirect. Inspect timing for the selected-PC mux feedback and the instruction-memory path.')\ns=s.replace('The timing benefit is unmeasured and low confidence:', 'The final unconditional-capture variant is unmeasured and low confidence:')\ns=s.replace('Earlier registered-recovery probes inserted latency;', 'An initial version with sticky selection and redirect-dependent recovery-register enable passed existing fetch/pipeline tests and canonical CoreMark, and reached 91.395 MHz median (about 2 percent higher fitness), below acceptance. The proposed unconditional-capture version removes that remaining live-redirect enable. Earlier registered-recovery probes inserted latency;')\nd['hypothesis']=s\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
