{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_69", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r13s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Bank speculative and correction PCs behind a registered recovery selector'\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace the single fetch PC register with two 32-bit candidate-address registers and one registered recovery selector. Keep all address bits; do not narrow or mask addresses. normal_pc_q holds the normal predictor-selected successor; recovery_pc_q holds the EX correction address. The active PC is recovering_q ? recovery_pc_q : normal_pc_q, and must drive every existing PC use: imem_addr, raw_fetch.pc, agree-table indexing, direct-target generation and PC+4. Capture normal_pc_q only on the existing fetch_accept, independently of redirect. Capture recovery_pc_q whenever \"'!recovering_q || redirect. Set recovering_q on redirect, otherwise clear it on fetch_accept, otherwise hold it. Reset normal_pc_q to RESET_PC and recovering_q to zero. The inactive recovery payload needs no reset: place its write outside the reset arm and let the reset selector make it unobservable. Every transition that selects it must capture the new redirect_target on that same edge.\n\nOn a redirect edge, the freshly captured correction bank becomes active and the existing FIFO flush discards every younger entry. During an instruction-bus hold after recovery, recovering_q remains set and the correction bank holds its address. On the first subsequent fetch_accept, normal_pc_q captures next_pc computed from the active correction address while the selector clears. A redirect concurrent with fetch_accept overrides the selector and captures a new correction address; any simultaneous normal-bank write is harmless. Consecutive redirects, repeated resets, queue-full holds and simultaneous predictor training must reproduce the original external PC recurrence exactly: reset ? RESET_PC : redirect ? redirect_target : fetch_accept ? next_pc : old_pc.\n\nThis is one predictor-recovery storage change with no additional recovery cycle. It separates capture of normal and correction addresses so late recovery selection is represented by a one-bit register and a mux after the address registers. The recovery bank'\"'s enable still contains redirect, so do not claim complete isolation of branch resolution; the measured benefit must come from the changed fanout and placement of wide PC selection. No counter change, comparator rewrite, delayed training or unrelated arithmetic optimization belongs in this hypothesis.\n\nKeep the sixteen-entry binary agree predictor, PC[5:2] index, weakly-agree reset, saturating updates and pre-edge lookup semantics. Preserve predicted_taken association through the unchanged four-entry fixed-head FIFO, empty bypass and ID/EX. Preserve direct-branch and JAL prediction, sequential JALR, EX comparison/target/trap/forwarding equations, exact-once training on EX advancement, FENCE.I serialization, validity-only squashing, actual RVFI pc_next and recovery priority over unavailable imem. Keep nret=1, every external port, decoder legality, all memory/retirement invariants, both M-extension implementations and every contract file unchanged.\n\nExpect the original 3984075 marker-delimited CoreMark cycles. An initial banked version that reset both address banks passed existing fetch/pipeline tests and canonical CoreMark, and reached 91.395 MHz median across all three Gowin options versus the champion's 89.575 MHz. This approximately two-percent fitness gain is below acceptance. The final proposal removes only the unnecessary reset of the inactive correction payload; its timing is pending. Confidence is low because the extra PC mux, bank flops and routing can offset the benefit, and divider recurrence/EX forwarding become competing limits. Separate exploratory two-bit Boolean comparison, delayed-training, unconditional-bank-capture and word-address-bank variants reached 79.927, 86.966, 84.505 and 79.298 MHz median respectively; all are excluded from the final change.\n\nProve the active-PC recurrence equivalent to the original over arbitrary redirect targets, fetch accepts, stalls, reset and simultaneous events. Specifically check that an uninitialized recovery payload cannot reach any output after reset and that a held active recovery address cannot be overwritten. Retain existing predictor/FIFO conservation and pipeline assertions; add focused reset/stall/recovery interleavings and wrong-path memory tests. Require the unchanged full riscv-formal and RVFI cosimulation gates, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation with both bus stalls, and all three Gowin placements. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 235.17218 iterations/s, approximately 93.695 MHz at unchanged cycles. The initial measured gain does not meet this threshold and must not be presented as an accepted improvement.'''\nd['changes'][0]['description']='Replace the single PC register with full-width normal and correction address banks plus a registered selector, exactly as specified. Reset only the normal bank and selector; retain the correction bank while selected and stalled. Feed the selected PC consistently into all existing fetch/prediction uses. Preserve table, FIFO, validity, interfaces and recovery timing.'\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
