{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r4s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-003-r4s2\ntitle: \"A 16-entry agree predictor with validity-only recovery\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The previous 64-entry bimodal/JAL predictor reduced stalled CoreMark\n  cycles from 4491485 to 4201709, but its 48.244 MHz implementation lost\n  to the current 52.313 MHz, 116.47 iterations/second champion. That\n  predictor proposal cleared decoded control fields on recovery; the\n  current champion instead retains payload and controls and annuls only\n  validity, with side effects qualified locally. Preserve this timing\n  distinction when adding prediction. A software replay of the canonical\n  CoreMark ELF found 529588 conditional branches inside the markers.\n  A 16-entry ordinary bimodal table made 70409 direction errors; changing\n  its counters to learn agreement with a backward-taken/forward-not-taken\n  bias reduced this to 55134. That nearly matches the prior 64-entry\n  bimodal table's 51854 errors using one quarter of its counter storage.\n  Larger local-history predictors offered small extra savings, and tested\n  gshare variants were worse. This agree encoding addresses destructive\n  aliasing while keeping lookup, training and placement costs small.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one compact direct-control-flow predictor: a 16-entry agree table\n  of two-bit saturating counters indexed by fetch PC[5:2], plus early\n  prediction of legal JAL. For a legal conditional BRANCH, define its\n  static bias as instr[31]: backward branches biased taken, forward\n  branches biased not taken. The counter predicts whether the outcome\n  agrees with that bias, not the taken direction itself. Predict taken\n  when counter[1] == bias. Reset counters to weak agreement (2'b10),\n  which initially gives backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction.\n  Sharing a counter between opposite-direction branches is constructive\n  when both usually follow their own bias. JAL is always predicted taken.\n  Extract B/J immediates from raw imem_data and share one PC-relative\n  target adder. Apply taken prediction only to word-aligned dire\n…[5971 of 9043 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7acf7109a08f5c48]…\nrce-address, load-use comparison or ID payload-enable cones.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed tests for predictor warmup, both direction corrections,\n      branches aliasing 64 bytes apart, adjacent branches, early JAL,\n      ordinary JALR recovery, misaligned targets and reserved encodings.\n      Cover load-dependent branches, backend holds behind memory/division,\n      correction with imem stalled, reset and concurrent lookup/training.\n      Drive side-effecting raw instructions during unavailable or squashed\n      fetches. Check exact RVFI PCs, resolved successors, order, operands,\n      links and traps, and no wrong-path or repeated memory transactions.\n      Include colliding forward-not-taken and backward-taken branches\n      that should both train toward agreement, and direction reversals\n      that train toward disagreement. Require a warmed no-wait taken\n      loop to avoid control bubbles and a held branch to train exactly\n      once. Retain all existing checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
