m the EX timing path, letting clock-rate gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.25%): A 64-entry BHT plus early JAL cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.45% at nearly unchanged Fmax (5.514 MHz), but lost to iterative division because prediction left the combinational DIV/REM timing bottleneck intact.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (improvement, +11.77%): A narrow four-bit launch prefix lets quotient_q/remainder_q isolate the final restoring steps from sign c\n…[2686 of 5758 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a1b176938674591e]…\nal and left 136.72 iterations/s behind the parallel-divider variant's 152.61.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r6s0 (regression, +5.37%): Feeding RV32M directly from fully forwarded ID/EX operands bypasses AUIPC/immediate mux delay on multiply and divider-launch paths, raising probe median Fmax from 68.545 to 72.222 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles and divider latency.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r6s1 (regression, +4.49%): Registering MUL exposes the divider's normalization-to-prefix launch chain unless its prefix is prepared directly from raw operands, while the extra multiply stall raised probe CoreMark cycles by 2.09%, leaving the combined evaluated gain below the 4.6% acceptance threshold.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r6s2 (improvement, +5.62%): A 16-entry agree predictor plus early JAL complements the parallel radix-16 divider by removing direct-control-flow bubbles while preserving fully forwarded ID/EX operands and validity-only recovery, raising CoreMark throughput from 152.61 to 161.18 iterations/s.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
