as in.valid && !recovery_pending and use it for\nEX/MEM.valid, forwarding eligibility, mismatch detection, predictor training,\ndivide launch, divide wait/result consumption and divide-start bookkeeping.\nA wrong-path load/store never becomes a valid EX/MEM memory operation; a\nwrong-path DIV never starts the divider or stalls corrected fetch; and a\nwrong-path branch/JAL/JALR never trains or replaces recovery. Qualify the\nhazard unit'\"'s id_ex_valid input with the same registered annulment. Invalid\npayloads may retain nonzero controls, but have no architectural effects.\n\nThe pending cycle cannot have an older dmem stall: detection required EX\nadvancement and the resolving branch/jump now occupies MEM, so MEM is not\na memory operation. Assert this ordering property and that an annulled\nDIV cannot manufacture execute_wait. During pending, gate new mismatch\ndetection off, so pending naturally clears after one cycle; capture the\ncorrected instruction, not another bubble. If an older memory operation\nis stalled before detection, preserve the current branch and defer both\ntraining and recovery until release. Keep raw decode, register-file reads,\noperand muxes and payload enables independent of combinational recovery.\n\nPreserve exact architectural EX/MEM.pc_next, source values and jump-link\nresults, including PC+4 fallback and suppressed link writes for misalignment.\nPreserve saved-direction checking, all branch conditions, counter reset,\nPC indexing, current-counter updates and pre-edge read/write semantics.\nRetain nret=1 and all ports, decoder legality, memory handshakes, the fully\nforwarded ID/EX operand boundary, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic. No fixed test,\nformal bound or evaluation file may be weakened or modified.\n\nPredict seven percent higher fitness with low confidence; this is not a\nmeasured result. At the intended unchanged 4203513 cycles, acceptance above\n181.38 * 1.046 = 189.72348 iterations/second needs median Gowin Fmax above\n79.751 MHz. The address-overlay mux adds delay before instruction memory,\nand registered annulment gains fanout into EX; either may outweigh removal\nof late recovery control. The reported EX/IF/EX/ID path may actually be\nshared arithmetic, in which case this retiming may not help. Check full\nvendor paths and measure all three placements with marker-delimited cycles.\nRequire unchanged formal, cocotb, cosimulation, canonical CRC/UART and bus\nstall checks. Preserve the existing ten-cycle branch-to-divide liveness\nbound and accept only measured fitness improvement greater than 4.6 percent.\n'''\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Register EX mismatch as recovery_pending and capture correction PC\nunconditionally into recovery_pc. Wire these to IF's address overlay\nand EX annulment; qualify the hazard unit's EX-valid input. Remove\ncombinational recovery from ID. Preserve every top-level/RVFI port.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Select the effective fetch PC from the registered correction or the\nnormal PC register. Use it for imem address, instruction PC, agree\nlookup and all predicted-successor arithmetic. On acceptance capture\nits predicted successor; on stalled recovery retain the correction\nin the normal PC register so it survives pending clearing. Keep the\nexisting predictor policy and counter update protocol unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Add registered EX annulment and consistently qualify all side effects,\nincluding EX/MEM validity, forwarding, training, recovery and divider\nlaunch/wait/completion. Preserve actual successor, link and trap\nsemantics. Export existing mismatch and correction target to the new\nregisters; the mismatch must not steer target-register data or enable.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Remove redirect-based validity squash. On recovery detection allow\nthe speculative younger capture, which will be annulled in EX next\ncycle. During registered recovery capture the corrected fetch normally,\nsubject only to existing readiness, load-use and backend hold rules.\nRetain payload and fully forwarded operand capture semantics.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update the predictor scoreboard to distinguish detection, pending\nrecovery and annulled EX entries, preserving all architectural and\noperand-capture checks for surviving instructions. Assert effective\nfetch PC and table indexing, pending clearing, corrected-address\nretention across imem stalls, and absence of backend holds or divide\nlaunch during annulment. Exercise wrong-path loads, stores, DIV/REM,\nJAL/JALR and branches; both mismatch directions; adjacent transfers;\nload dependencies; older dmem holds; unavailable imem; alignment\ntraps; and reset during recovery. Check exact PC/order/operands/link\nresults, no wrong-path bus effects, unchanged prediction throughput\nand the unchanged ten-cycle branch-to-divide progress bound.\n'''}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,allow_unicode=False,width=85))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised and schema-validated recovery-overlay hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Revised and schema-validated recovery-overlay hypothesis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"command -v verilator; command -v riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc; ls /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh; sed -n '1,140p' tools/eval/cosim.py; sed -n '1,120p' tools/eval/fpga.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_21", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"command -v verilator; command -v riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc; ls /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh; sed -n '1,140p' tools/eval/cosim.py; sed -n '1,120p' tools/eval/fpga.py\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/verilator\n/srv/hwebench/toolchain/bin/riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc\n/opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\n\"\"\"Runs Verilator cosim for all bench ELFs. Returns structured pass/fail.\n\nCorrectness is gated in two stages:\n  1. Full RVFI trace cosim for selftest and any other small ELF — every\n     retirement is diffed against the Python reference ISS.\n  2. CoreMark CRC validation via the sim's UART capture — full-trace cosim\n     of coremark.elf is skipped because the Python reference is ~10⁴× slower\n     than Verilator (would take 30+ minutes per candidate). Instead we run\n     the sim in --bench mode and verify its UART matches the canonical CRCs\n     with `validate_coremark_uart` (the same check run_fpga_eval uses). This\n     catches any CPU bug that affects CoreMark output without the full trace\n     overhead.\n\"\"\"\nimport os, subprocess, json, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\nfrom tools.eval.formal import read_nret\nfrom tools.eval._subprocess import run_pgroup\n\nBENCH_DIR = Path(\"bench/programs\")\n\n\ndef _build_cosim_env(worktree, target: str | None,\n                     base_env: dict | None = None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Build the env for `bash test/cosim/build.sh`.\n\n    Reads cores/<target>/core.yaml's `nret` (defaults to 2) and injects\n    NRET=<n> so build.sh can pass `-DNRET=<n>` to verilator. main.cpp's\n    channel-1 drain is compiled out under `#if NRET < 2`, so a single-\n    issue core (no `_1` ports) builds cleanly.\n    \"\"\"\n    worktree_path = Path(worktree).resolve()\n    env = dict(base_env) if base_env is not None else os.environ.copy()\n    if target is None:\n        return env\n    env[\"RTL_DIR\"] = f\"cores/{target}/rtl\"\n    env[\"OBJ_DIR\"] = f\"cores/{target}/obj_dir\"\n    env[\"NRET\"] = str(read_nret(worktree_path / \"cores\" / target / \"core.yaml\"))\n    return env\n\n# Stall modes for the full-trace comparison. The stalled mode uses the same\n# bus backpressure as the fitness run, so logic that only acts while a\n# memory port stalls (whi\n…[9051 of 12123 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5bf908d303200c69]…\n/*\n    # as worktree-relative paths. Without cwd, it would read from the caller's cwd\n    # (e.g., repo root) and we'd score the wrong design.\n    proc = await asyncio.create_subprocess_exec(\n        \"bash\", NEXTPNR_SCRIPT, str(seed), outdir,\n        stdout=asyncio.subprocess.PIPE,\n        stderr=asyncio.subprocess.PIPE,\n        cwd=Path(worktree).resolve(),\n        env=env,\n        start_new_session=True,  # own process group, so a timeout kills nextpnr too\n    )\n    try:\n        stdout, stderr = await asyncio.wait_for(proc.communicate(),\n                                                timeout=NEXTPNR_TIMEOUT_SEC)\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        try:\n            os.killpg(proc.pid, 9)\n        except ProcessLookupError:\n            pass\n        await proc.wait()\n        return {\n            'seed': seed,\n            'fmax_mhz': None,\n            'log': f'nextpnr timeout after {NEXTPNR_TIMEOUT_SEC}s',\n            'returncode': None,\n            'placement_failed': True,\n            'timed_out': True,\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
