{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r8s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r8s2',\n 'title':'Queue agree-predictor training and update counters locally',\n 'category':'predictor',\n 'motivation':'''The successful sixteen-entry agree predictor already captures nearly all\nof the cycle benefit measured for the larger BHT: 4203513 versus 4201709\nCoreMark cycles. The current training circuit reads both counter bitplanes\nthrough a dynamic index, computes a centralized saturating update, and\nroutes that result back through an indexed write. EX branch resolution\nalso drives recovery and misalignment handling. The champion's full Gowin\npath includes branch-comparison carry cells, cells named train_next, and\ntrap-adjusted forwarding eligibility before returning to ID/EX operands.\nThe names reflect synthesis sharing, not necessarily a literal RTL path\nthrough the predictor. This motivates isolating training and replacing its\nglobal read-modify-write network with independent counter feedback cells.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the current sixteen-entry, two-bit agree policy, backward/forward\nbias, aligned direct-target generation, early JAL and saved-direction\nrecovery. Change only the training microarchitecture: put a one-cycle\nregistered training record between EX resolution and the table, then update\nthe selected counter using its own local state. The record contains valid,\nfour-bit index and agree outcome. It accepts one resolution per cycle and\nis consumed unconditionally on the following edge, so consecutive branches\ncannot overflow it. Reset clears record validity and resets counters to 10.\nCapture the index/outcome every cycle; train_valid determines relevance.\n\nThe existing EX train_valid remains qualified by valid legal conditional\nbranch advancement, \"'!reset, !stall_ex_mem and no target-alignment fault.\nA held branch enqueues nothing until it advances. A bubble clears the next\nrecord'\"'s valid bit while a previous valid record may still update its\ncounter. Recovery must not discard an already-resolved older branch's\nqueued update; a squashed younger branch must never enqueue. No stall,\nbackpressure or new frontend control dependency may be added for training.\n\nImplement sixteen local two-flop counter cells, retaining the existing\nagree_msb_q and agree_lsb_q bitplanes if useful for diagnostics. Decode the\nregistered update index to a per-cell write enable; do not multiplex a\ncounter value into a central arithmetic unit. For old state {m,l}, an agree\nupdate is {m|l, m|\"'!l}; a disagree update is {m&l, m&!l}. Both bits use the\nold state on the edge. These are exactly saturating increment/decrement\nfor all four states, including 00 and 11. An unselected cell holds state.\nThe predictor lookup remains the asynchronous MSB read indexed by fetch\nPC[5:2], with the existing comparison to the branch-immediate sign bit.\n\nThere is no same-cycle lookup bypass from either the incoming or queued\ntraining record. Fetch sees pre-edge table state and captures the exact\nprediction it used with its instruction. The queued update uses the\ncurrent local counter, never a counter value saved at branch fetch or\nresolution. Thus consecutive same-index updates accumulate in order,\nincluding across saturation, stalls and intervening corrections. Training\nis delayed by one cycle relative to the champion; an adjacent aliased\nbranch may see an older counter, affecting accuracy only. Do not change\nbranch/jump target arithmetic, redirect qualification, ID payload enables,\nvalidity-only squash, forwarding or any divider/multiply logic.\n\nThe expected six-percent fitness gain is low confidence and unmeasured.\nThe benefit sought is Fmax from separating branch-outcome fanout and removing\nthe table'\"'s global read-modify-write path, not an accuracy improvement.\nAdded update-register fanout and altered placement may instead lose; the\nunchanged divider recurrence is already nearly as slow as the worst path.\nMeasure marker-delimited CoreMark cycles because delayed training can\nchange the miss sequence and bus-stall phase. At 4203513 cycles, acceptance\nabove 181.38 * 1.046 = 189.72348 iterations/second needs median Fmax above\n79.750512 MHz. Require all three Gowin placements and greater than 4.6\npercent measured fitness improvement, not an isolated placement win.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and all top-level/RVFI ports, exact architectural pc_next,\nall trap/decoder invariants, counter indexing and static bias, branch\nalignment behavior, memory bounds/handshakes, exact-once effects, the\nexisting ten-cycle branch-to-divide progress bound, and real/ALTOPS M\narithmetic. Run unchanged formal, pipeline/counter checks, cosimulation\nand canonical CRC/UART validation. Do not alter any evaluation contract.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add a one-cycle training record with resettable valid/index/agree\nregisters and no backpressure. Replace indexed central counter\nread-modify-write with sixteen per-entry saturating feedback cells\nusing the registered record and current local state. Preserve normal\nprediction reads, pre-edge semantics, predictor capacity, bias,\ntarget formation, fetch acceptance and recovery priority.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update the predictor scoreboard to model the one-cycle training queue\nand distinguish EX enqueue from table application without relaxing\narchitectural, prediction-capture or recovery checks. Exhaust all\ncounter states and both outcomes. Cover consecutive same-index and\ndifferent-index updates, saturation, alternating outcomes, a queued\nupdate during a later backend stall, imem unavailability, recovery,\nand reset. Verify no stale-counter overwrite, duplicate held-branch\nupdate, dropped valid record or wrong-path training. Retain every\nRVFI/order/memory/CRC and branch-to-divide progress assertion.\n'''}]\n}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent_str(dumper, data):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=85))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote schema-valid queued-training hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
