{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r7s2','r6s2')):\n   h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n   print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Early direct-branch prediction with a 64-entry bimodal table regression 13.12 5.514 4201709\nhyp-20261002-003-r2s2 Compact early branch prediction on the iterative-divider champion regression 91.19 38.315 4201709\nhyp-20261002-003-r3s2 Early branch prediction with recovery isolated from decode data regression 114.82 48.244 4201709\nhyp-20261002-003-r4s2 A 16-entry agree predictor with validity-only recovery regression 115.14 48.401 4203513\nhyp-20261002-003-r5s2 Compact agree prediction with registered EX operands regression 136.72 57.47 4203513\nhyp-20261002-003-r6s2 Compact agree prediction on the parallel-divider champion improvement 161.18 67.751 4203513\nAdd one early direct-control-flow predictor to the supplied champion:\na 16-entry two-bit agree table indexed by fetch PC[5:2], including early\ndirect JAL prediction. Preserve the current parallel radix-16 divider,\nregistered divisor multiples, t8 completion handshake, fully forwarded\nID/EX operands, and syn_ramstyle = \"registers\" register-file storage.\nThis is a predictor-only change; do not transplant an older core or add\na BTB, return stack, global history, cache, pipeline stage, or ALU change.\n\nStore the 32 learned bits as two 16-bit counter bitplanes. For a legal\nconditional branch, use instr[31] as the static taken bias: backward\ntaken, forward not taken. Reset each counter to weak agreement (2'b10).\nPredict taken when its counter MSB equals that branch's instr[31].\nIncrement toward 2'b11 when the resolved direction agrees with the\nresolving branch's imm[31], and decrement toward 2'b00 otherwise.\nUpdate the current stored counter at the resolving PC[5:2]; do not\noverwrite it from an old lookup snapshot. Use pre-edge state for a\nsimultaneous lookup and update, without an EX-to-fetch update bypass.\n\nPredecode raw imem_data independently of recovery and readiness.\nRecognize BRANCH only for funct3 in {0,1,4,5,6,7}; recognize JAL by its\nexact opcode and predict it taken unconditionally. Extract the B/J\nimmediate and use one shared PC-relative\n…[7202 of 10274 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8b09a3c1b6fbc273]…\nse overrides an\nunavailable younger fetch. Do not add prediction-table capacity, a BTB,\na return stack, extra stages, or speculation requiring rollback.\n\nThis is a timing hypothesis with unchanged intended CoreMark cycle\ncount. It needs median Gowin Fmax above approximately 70.87 MHz at\n4203513 cycles to exceed the 168.59428 iterations/second acceptance\nthreshold. The expected five-percent gain is low confidence, not a\nmeasured improvement: extra endpoint registers and fetch fanout may\ninstead hurt placement, and the existing arithmetic path may dominate.\nReject the candidate unless measured Fmax times marker-delimited\niterations/cycle improves by more than 4.6 percent. Use unchanged\nformal, cocotb, cosimulation, CRC, UART, and both bus-stall checks.\nPreserve nret=1, every top-level port, decoder legality, memory behavior,\nthe radix-16 divider and ALTOPS behavior, and all contract files.\n\nhyp-20261002-003-r8s2 Isolate branch-predictor resolution from EX forwarding eligibility regression 155.78 65.483 4203513\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"The history shows little benefit from a larger direction table, and both recent branch-resolution timing changes regressed. JALR remains unpredicted. I’m focusing on return prediction to avoid flushing the fetch queue at function returns."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-003-r9s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict function returns with an EX-updated return-address stack\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current sixteen-entry agree predictor and early JAL already capture\n  nearly all the measured benefit of the earlier 64-entry direction table.\n  The r7s2 and r8s2 attempts to rearrange branch-resolution timing both\n  regressed. Every JALR still predicts PC+4, so ordinary function returns\n  force recovery and discard the four-entry fetch queue. Return prediction\n  targets this remaining control-flow cost without changing the arithmetic\n  pipeline or forwarding boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one eight-entry return-address stack (RAS), updated only when a valid,\n  legal, aligned call or return advances from EX. Use it in raw fetch to\n  predict canonical returns: legal JALR with rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5, and zero\n  immediate. Keep the current agree predictor, direct-target adder, early\n  JAL, four-entry fixed-head queue and empty bypass unchanged. Other JALRs\n  remain sequentially predicted. Predict only with a nonempty stack and a\n  word-aligned top entry. Use registered stack state, with no same-cycle\n  EX-to-IF update bypass and no speculative stack mutation on fetch.\n\n  Store full architectural return addresses as a fixed-top flop stack.\n  Push PC+4 for aligned JAL/JALR calls writing x1 or x5. For legal JALR use\n  the standard x1/x5 hint table: pop when only rs1 is a link register, push\n  when only rd is a link register, push without pop when rd=rs1 is a link\n  register, and pop then push when both are distinct link registers.\n  JAL writing another register has no stack action. Saturate occupancy at\n  eight, discard the oldest entry on overflow, and make underflow inert.\n  On pop-then-push, replace the previous top; when empty, push normally.\n  Apply each action exactly once on EX advancement, including correctly\n  predicted controls; dmem holds must not repeat it. Reset clears occupancy.\n  Resolving older controls are the only writers, so wrong-path fetches,\n  branch recovery and FENCE.I need no stack rollback. A lagging stack can\n  reduce accuracy but must never change architectural execution.\n\n  Carry the actual predicted return target alongside predicted_taken in\n  each queue entry and ID/EX payload; do not reconstruct a prediction from\n  the current stack at decode or resolution. Preserve it through backend\n  and load-use holds. Direct controls keep the existing direction-only\n  check and exact immediate targets. For JALR, EX must detect BOTH a\n  direction mismatch and a taken prediction with the wrong target. Compare\n  the captured full target with the architectural (rs1+imm)&~1 successor.\n  For JALR recovery select the actual aligned target, or PC+4 on an alignment\n  trap, independently of predicted_taken: the old taken-bit recovery mux\n  is insufficient for a wrong taken target. Leave direct-control recovery,\n  FENCE.I serialization, trap/link-write suppression and actual RVFI pc_next\n  semantics intact. A predicted return resolving to a misaligned target\n  must trap, annul the prediction and recover sequentially.\n\n  Keep recovery and predictor updates qualified by valid, legality, \"'!reset\n  and EX advancement. Recovery flushes all younger queue entries and annuls\n  ID/EX validity, overriding unavailable imem; older dmem holds defer it.\n  Neither recovery nor prediction may gate raw instruction payload, source\n  addresses, operand forwarding, or arithmetic data paths. Preserve nret=1,\n  the existing top-level interfaces, decoder policy, real and ALTOPS M\n  behavior, all memory bounds and every verification invariant.\n\n  Predict six percent higher fitness with low confidence, not a measured\n  result. The gain depends on return frequency and accuracy despite fetch\n  running ahead of the EX-updated stack. Extra stack and target metadata,\n  target comparison and routing can reduce Fmax; do not add speculative\n  checkpoints or a general target cache to this hypothesis. Validate with\n  unchanged formal, cocotb and RVFI cosimulation plus canonical CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks under both bus-stall streams. Compare the median\n  Gowin Fmax times iterations/cycle against 191.27; acceptance requires\n  strictly more than 200.06842 iterations/second, not merely fewer cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add an eight-entry fixed-top flop RAS and EX-qualified update inputs.\n      Predict canonical x1/x5 returns from its registered top on raw fetch,\n      save the target in raw_fetch and every FIFO entry, and preserve the\n      existing direct predictor, queue acceptance and redirect priority.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a full-width predicted indirect target payload to if_id_t and\n      id_ex_t, retaining predicted_taken, leading operand fields and the\n      final valid field. No downstream architectural or RVFI field changes.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the saved predicted target with its instruction under the\n      existing payload enable and retain it across holds. Keep all operand\n      capture, validity-only squashing and decoding behavior intact.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Emit once-per-advancing-control RAS push/pop actions and PC+4 data.\n      Add JALR-only target-mismatch recovery against the captured prediction\n      and select its actual successor even when both directions are taken.\n      Preserve direct-control resolution, FENCE.I, trap-adjusted forwarding,\n      jump link values, divider and architectural next-PC reporting.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire EX stack-update action and return-address payload to IF while\n      preserving the external memory and single-channel RVFI port set.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add nested and repeated calls/returns, alternate x5 links, all JALR\n      hint actions, stack overflow/underflow, wrong return targets, tail\n      calls, short callees and back-to-back returns. Check noncanonical and\n      illegal JALR, misalignment, reset, wrong-path calls, full-queue recovery,\n      load-dependent returns, dmem holds and unavailable imem. Verify exact\n      retirement PCs, actual successors, links, operands, traps, order and\n      absence of wrong-path stores; check once-only updates and a warmed\n      correctly predicted return issuing without a recovery bubble.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the queue-aware predictor reference for registered RAS updates\n      and saved target metadata, including simultaneous lookup/update,\n      fetch/decode decoupling, accepted responses, reset, occupancy limits\n      and flushes. Retain the existing queue and direct-predictor assertions.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml'\nh=yaml.safe_load(open(p))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-003-r9s2\ntitle: \\\"Predict function returns with an EX-updated return-address stack\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current sixteen-entry agree predictor and early JAL already capture\n  nearly all the measured benefit of the earlier 64-entry direction table.\n  The r7s2 and r8s2 attempts to rearrange branch-resolution timing both\n  regressed. Every JALR still predicts PC+4, so ordinary function returns\n  force recovery and discard the four-entry fetch queue. Return prediction\n  targets this remaining control-flow cost without changing the arithmetic\n  pipeline or forwarding boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one eight-entry return-address stack (RAS), updated only when a valid,\n  legal, aligned call or return advances from EX. Use it in raw fetch to\n  predict canonical returns: legal JALR with rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5, and zero\n  immediate. Keep the current agree predictor, direct-target adder, early\n  JAL, four-entry fixed-head queue and empty bypass unchanged. Other JALRs\n  remain sequentially predicted. Predict only with a nonempty stack and a\n  word-aligned top entry. Use registered stack state, with no same-cycle\n  EX-to-IF update bypass and no speculative stack mutation on fetch.\n\n  Store full architectural return addresses as a fixed-top flop stack.\n  Push PC+4 for aligned JAL/JALR calls writing x1 or x5. For legal JALR use\n  the standard x1/x5 hint table: pop when only rs1 is a link register, push\n  when only rd is a link register, push without pop when rd=rs1 is a link\n  register, and pop then push when both are distinct link registers.\n  JAL writing another register has no stack action. Saturate occupancy at\n  eight, discard the oldest entry on overflow, and make underflow inert.\n  On pop-then-push, replace the previous top; when empty, push normally.\n  Apply each action exactly once on EX advancement, including correctly\n  predicted controls; dmem holds must not repeat it. Reset clears occupancy.\n  Resolving older controls are the only writers, so wrong-path fetches,\n  branch recovery and FENCE.I need no stack rollback. A lagging stack can\n  reduce accuracy but must never change architectural execution.\n\n  Carry the actual predicted return target alongside predicted_taken in\n  each queue entry and ID/EX payload; do not reconstruct a prediction from\n  the current stack at decode or resolution. Preserve it through backend\n  and load-use holds. Direct controls keep the existing direction-only\n  check and exact immediate targets. For JALR, EX must detect BOTH a\n  direction mismatch and a taken prediction with the wrong target. Compare\n  the captured full target with the architectural (rs1+imm)&~1 successor.\n  For JALR recovery select the actual aligned target, or PC+4 on an alignment\n  trap, independently of predicted_taken: the old taken-bit recovery mux\n  is insufficient for a wrong taken target. Leave direct-control recovery,\n  FENCE.I serialization, trap/link-write suppression and actual RVFI pc_next\n  semantics intact. A predicted return resolving to a misaligned target\n  must trap, annul the prediction and recover sequentially.\n\n  Keep recovery and predictor updates qualified by valid, legality, \"'!reset\n  and EX advancement. Recovery flushes all younger queue entries and annuls\n  ID/EX validity, overriding unavailable imem; older dmem holds defer it.\n  Neither recovery nor prediction may gate raw instruction payload, source\n  addresses, operand forwarding, or arithmetic data paths. Preserve nret=1,\n  the existing top-level interfaces, decoder policy, real and ALTOPS M\n  behavior, all memory bounds and every verification invariant.\n\n  Predict six percent higher fitness with low confidence, not a measured\n  result. The gain depends on return frequency and accuracy despite fetch\n  running ahead of the EX-updated stack. Extra stack and target metadata,\n  target comparison and routing can reduce Fmax; do not add speculative\n  checkpoints or a general target cache to this hypothesis. Validate with\n  unchanged formal, cocotb and RVFI cosimulation plus canonical CoreMark\n  CRC/UART/marker checks under both bus-stall streams. Compare the median\n  Gowin Fmax times iterations/cycle against 191.27; acceptance requires\n  strictly more than 200.06842 iterations/second, not merely fewer cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add an eight-entry fixed-top flop RAS and EX-qualified update inputs.\n      Predict canonical x1/x5 returns from its registered top on raw fetch,\n      save the target in raw_fetch and every FIFO entry, and preserve the\n      existing direct predictor, queue acceptance and redirect priority.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a full-width predicted indirect target payload to if_id_t and\n      id_ex_t, retaining predicted_taken, leading operand fields and the\n      final valid field. No downstream architectural or RVFI field changes.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the saved predicted target with its instruction under the\n      existing payload enable and retain it across holds. Keep all operand\n      capture, validity-only squashing and decoding behavior intact.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Emit once-per-advancing-control RAS push/pop actions and PC+4 data.\n      Add JALR-only target-mismatch recovery against the captured prediction\n      and select its actual successor even when both directions are taken.\n      Preserve direct-control resolution, FENCE.I, trap-adjusted forwarding,\n      jump link values, divider and architectural next-PC reporting.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire EX stack-update action and return-address payload to IF while\n      preserving the external memory and single-channel RVFI port set.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add nested and repeated calls/returns, alternate x5 links, all JALR\n      hint actions, stack overflow/underflow, wrong return targets, tail\n      calls, short callees and back-to-back returns. Check noncanonical and\n      illegal JALR, misalignment, reset, wrong-path calls, full-queue recovery,\n      load-dependent returns, dmem holds and unavailable imem. Verify exact\n      retirement PCs, actual successors, links, operands, traps, order and\n      absence of wrong-path stores; check once-only updates and a warmed\n      correctly predicted return issuing without a recovery bubble.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the queue-aware predictor reference for registered RAS updates\n      and saved target metadata, including simultaneous lookup/update,\n      fetch/decode decoupling, accepted responses, reset, occupancy limits\n      and flushes. Retain the existing queue and direct-predictor assertions.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml'\nh=yaml.safe_load(open(p))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Validated cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
